#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bonsai2-manager — MAP 2.1–2.11 / S2.1–S2.8: the subagent instance manager.

Runs from `bonsai2-sglang.service` (Type=notify via this file's sd_notify;
S2.2). It is the actor for the whole S2 lifecycle:

  * reads `/opt/bonsai2/etc/subagent-engine.yaml` + `serving.yaml` (S2.2)
  * runs the S2.3 boot-time budget check —
        `N' = max(0, min(N, floor(free_gb / per_instance_budget_gb)))`
    with `free_gb` read from the MAIN container's live cgroup
    `memory.current` (sleep-accurate) else the recorded fresh-load
    `main_stack.footprint_gb` fallback (S2.3)
  * runs the S2.4 N=0 PTQ1_0 fallback (MAP 2.7): when the S2.3 formula
    yields N' = 0 at the PQ2_0 budget, re-plan once at the PTQ1_0
    fallback footprint (~18% smaller, S1.1) + the S2.4 ctx-halve retry
    on an allocation-tier fail (multiple of 8192, floor 8192); records
    the quant in use in `subagent-engine.yaml` (S2.4)
  * runs the S1.4 allocation tier before each spawn — the stricter
    `free >= instance_footprint_GB + 1.0` gate, with the bounded free
    read (5 s probe + one retry, then free = 0) (S1.4/S2.3)
  * spawns the granted instances IN ORDER via `systemctl start
    bonsai2-server@i`, waiting each to healthy before the next (S2.5);
    boot order is main-stack-first (S2.1 — enforced by the unit's
    `After=qwen38-sglang.service`; the manager re-checks main health
    and records `main_healthy`)
  * writes `/run/bonsai2/health.json` in the S3.1 schema (S2.2), atomically
  * sends `READY=1` to systemd once the boot sequence completes (Type=notify)
  * then runs the steady health-probe loop (S2.2) with:
      - the S2.2 escalation (MAP 2.6): two consecutive probe fails ->
        `degraded`; five -> a `systemctl restart` attempt (manager
        backstop on top of the unit's `Restart=on-failure` — a unit
        systemd already restarted since the fail streak began is
        deferred to, not double-restarted)
      - the S2.5 drift watch (MAP 2.8): cgroup pressure sustained > 30 s
        over the instance's `--memory` cap, evaluated on the 5-minute
        recheck -> halve that instance's ctx in its unit + `systemctl
        restart` in place; `ctx_reduced: true` + the new ctx in
        health.json; counted in the instance's restart budget; `bunloaded`
        instances are never re-spawned
      - the S3.2 unload/reload manager-level semantics (MAP 2.10): the
        `bonsai2-unload` / `bonsai2-reload` commands (Phase 3) write
        `control/unload` / `control/reload` JSON into the health dir;
        the manager processes them each cycle (unload -> `systemctl
        stop` + `bunloaded` + `user_unloaded` main-only reason; reload ->
        S1.4 tier + unit start + health re-probe, partial on a tier fail)
        and writes a timestamped ack file the command can poll (dead
        manager = no ack = the Phase-3 exit-84 path)

MAP scope for this file: 2.1 (skeleton: notify + config + health.json),
2.2 (boot-time budget check), 2.3 (allocation tier), 2.5 (spawn path),
2.6 (S2.2 escalation: 2-fail -> degraded, 5-fail -> systemctl restart,
NRestarts dedup against systemd's own Restart=on-failure),
2.7 (S2.4 N=0/main-only: the PTQ1_0 fallback retry + the S2.4 ctx-halve
retry + the quant/ctx bookkeeping in subagent-engine.yaml),
2.8 (S2.5 drift watch: 5-min recheck, 30 s sustained cgroup pressure ->
in-place ctx halve + unit restart), 2.9 (S2.6: the measured boot written
back to serving.yaml boot_target_s), 2.10 (the S3.2 unload/reload
manager-level semantics the Phase-3 commands mediate through the
control/ request queue). 2.4 is the unit files
(`units/`, the manager is what they run); 2.11 is the S2.8 acceptance
run (tests/gpu/test_lifecycle_2_11.py + the run itself).

Stdlib-only for the core so it is hermetic-testable; PyYAML at the YAML
edge (same convention as the other `bin/` tools). sd_notify is hand-rolled
(AF_UNIX datagram per the systemd NotifySocket protocol) so the manager
has no systemd-Python dependency. `bonsai2_vram` (the S1.4 pure formula)
is imported from the same `bin/` dir for the footprint term.

Exit codes (the manager PROCESS; S1.4 / S2.4 / S2.3):
  0 = boot complete — full load, partial load (allocation short at i > 0,
      the S1.4 "i > 0 partial load, exit 0" rule), or main-only (N' = 0,
      the S2.4 handoff, exit 0)
  4 = ERR_VRAM_OOM — the S2.3 planning tier granted N' >= 1 but the S1.4
      allocation tier failed at i = 0 (VRAM was lost *after* the budget
      ran — "granted at plan time, short at allocation time"). This is a
      BOOT-TIME failure, handled BEFORE `READY=1` is sent: the health
      document is written (0 instances, stack derived degraded), a
      STATUS= is emitted, then a STOPPING= (pre-READY exit bookkeeping,
      G3 PR #10 r10 P2), and the process exits 4 so systemd sees a
      FAILED START under `Type=notify` and applies
      `Restart=on-failure` / `RestartSec=5` (the S1.4 "the manager exits
      4 before the instance's KV allocation" contract — S1.4's exit 4
      IS the unit-level signal here).
  2 = ERR_CONFIG — a YAML failed to parse / is malformed, OR a boot-tier
      memory read (MemTotal / MemAvailable) failed (the /proc/meminfo
      OSErr -> ERR_CONFIG mapping; see main())

Under `Type=notify` the full/partial/main-only boots send `READY=1` and
the process then STAYS ALIVE in the steady probe loop until systemd
stops it (a stopped unit exits 0; the boot-time exit 4 above is the
only exit code the unit ever observes without a prior READY).
`--settle N` exits 0 after N seconds for the @gpu harness / manual runs.

Usage:
  bonsai2-manager                 # systemd Type=notify daemon (run forever)
  bonsai2-manager --settle 45     # boot, then probe-loop for 45 s, exit 0
  bonsai2-manager --config-dir D  # read the two YAMLs from D (default
                                  #   /opt/bonsai2/etc)
Env overrides (hermetic/test hooks; the S3.2-style convention):
  BONSAI2_ENGINE_YAML   full path to subagent-engine.yaml
  BONSAI2_SERVING_YAML  full path to serving.yaml
  BONSAI2_HEALTH_DIR    dir for health.json (default /run/bonsai2)
  BONSAI2_USER_SYSTEMCTL=1   prefix systemctl with `--user` (user-scope
                        units; the @gpu test harness. System-scope is the
                        install-time default — the S2.2 units are
                        system units in production.)
  BONSAI2_HEALTH_POLL_S   override the steady-loop probe cadence (the
                        serving.yaml `health_poll_s` value when unset or
                        non-positive). Test hook, docs/two-red-gpu-
                        tests-fix.md + docs/pr11-two-red-gpu-tests.md.
  BONSAI2_PROBE_TIMEOUT_S  cap the probe's socket timeout so a dead probe
                        fails fast instead of occupying the cycle. Test
                        hook, docs/two-red-gpu-tests-fix.md.
  BONSAI2_DRIFT_RECHECK_S  override the S2.5 drift recheck cadence.
  BONSAI2_DRIFT_PRESSURE_S  override the S2.5 sustained-pressure window.

"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.request
from pathlib import Path

# Reuse the S1.4 pure formula (MAP 1.6) for the allocation-tier footprint
# term. Both live in the same bin/ dir (the manager is deployed to
# /opt/bonsai2/bin/bonsai2-manager; the formula is
# /opt/bonsai2/bin/bonsai2_vram.py), so make our own dir importable.
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
import bonsai2_vram

EXIT_OK = 0
EXIT_CONFIG = 2
EXIT_VRAM_OOM = 4  # S1.4 ERR_VRAM_OOM (the i = 0 allocation-tier defeat)

# S1.4 allocation-tier spike margin (GB): `free >= footprint + 1.0`.
TIER_MARGIN_GB = 1.0
# S1.4: kv_cache dtype -> per_slot_per_token_GB (SPEC S1.4 'formula,
# locked'). Only q4_0 has a LOCKED measured constant (the S1.3/S1.4
# re-anchor, MAP 1.7: 6.87 GB KV at 8 slots x 262144 ctx on this box).
# G3 PR#11 r1: the table lives in bonsai2_vram (the S1.4 formula
# module, single source of truth); the @gpu acceptance tests read the
# constants from there too. The S1.6 `kv_cache` key now feeds the S1.4
# footprint through load_config (G3 PR #10 r13 P1: a non-q4_0 edit must
# not silently gate VRAM on the q4_0 constant — it has no locked value,
# so it is an ERR_CONFIG at load, not a guess at load time).
KV_PER_SLOT_PER_TOKEN_GB = bonsai2_vram.KV_PER_SLOT_PER_TOKEN_GB
# S1.4 bounded free read: 5 s per probe, one retry, then free = 0.
FREE_PROBE_TIMEOUT_S = 5.0
FREE_PROBE_RETRIES = 1

DEFAULT_CONFIG_DIR = "/opt/bonsai2/etc"
DEFAULT_HEALTH_DIR = "/run/bonsai2"

# S2.2 health-probe constants (the steady-loop values; the S2.2
# escalation (MAP 2.6) is driven off these).
HEALTH_PROBE_POLL_S = 2.0
COMPLETION_RT_MAX_S = 10.0  # S2.2: a 16-token completion must RT < 10 s
COMPLETION_MAX_TOKENS = 16

# S2.2 escalation (MAP 2.6): `Two consecutive fails -> degraded;
# five -> restart attempt.` — the probe cadence is health_poll_s, so
# "consecutive fails" = consecutive health_poll_s cycles in which the
# S2.2 probe (models + 16-token completion) failed.
DEGRADED_FAILS = 2
RESTART_FAILS = 5
# After a manager-issued restart, the unit goes through ExecStartPre +
# the 27 B cold load; systemd's `Restart=on-failure` / the unit itself
# owns that window. If the unit restarted on its own since the fail
# streak began, the manager defers to it (no double restart) — see
# `_refresh_states_with_escalation`.

# S2.5 drift watch (MAP 2.8): `re-checks every 5 min; if an instance's
# cgroup memory pressure is sustained (> 30 s over its --memory cap),
# halve its ctx + restart in place`.
DRIFT_RECHECK_S = 300.0  # env BONSAI2_DRIFT_RECHECK_S overrides (tests)
DRIFT_PRESSURE_S = 30.0  # env BONSAI2_DRIFT_PRESSURE_S overrides (tests)
# S2.5: `halve, rounded down to a multiple of 8192, floor 8192`.
CTX_HALVE_MULTIPLE = 8192
CTX_FLOOR = 8192


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# sd_notify (Type=notify, S2.2) — hand-rolled, no systemd python dep.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
def notify_sd(state: str) -> None:
    """Send `state` (e.g. "READY=1", "STATUS=...") to systemd.

    Implements the systemd NotifySocket protocol: an AF_UNIX datagram to
    the socket path in $NOTIFY_SOCKET (an abstract socket when the path
    starts with '@'). No-op when NOTIFY_SOCKET is unset (the manager is
    not running under systemd — the @gpu test harness path).
    """
    path = os.environ.get("NOTIFY_SOCKET")
    if not path:
        return
    try:
        if path.startswith("@"):
            # Abstract socket: strip the '@', prepend the NUL byte.
            sock = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX, socket.SOCK_DGRAM)
            sock.sendto(state.encode(), ("\0" + path[1:],))
            sock.close()
        else:
            sock = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX, socket.SOCK_DGRAM)
            sock.sendto(state.encode(), path)
            sock.close()
    except OSError:
        # sd_notify is advisory; a broken NOTIFY_SOCKET (a non-systemd
        # run) must never kill the manager's boot sequence.
        _log(f"sd_notify: could not send {state!r} to {path!r} (ignored)")


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# logging (stderr = human-only; stdout is reserved for one JSON on exit,
# the S3.1 contract applied to the manager's own terminal report)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
def _log(msg: str) -> None:
    ts = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S")
    print(f"[{ts}] bonsai2-manager: {msg}", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# config loading (S2.2) — PyYAML at the edge, stdlib core
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
def _load_yaml_map(path: Path) -> dict:
    import yaml  # PyYAML: box dep (edge-only, same convention as MAP 1.5)

    text = path.read_text()
    data = yaml.safe_load(text)
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        raise ValueError(f"ERR_CONFIG: {path}: top level is not a mapping")  # noqa: TRY004
    return data


def load_config(engine_yaml: Path, serving_yaml: Path) -> dict:
    """Load + lightly-normalize the two S2.2 config files.

    Raises ValueError (ERR_CONFIG) on a missing/malformed file or a
    missing required key, so the caller maps it to exit 2.
    """
    for p in (engine_yaml, serving_yaml):
        if not p.is_file():
            raise ValueError(f"ERR_CONFIG: config not found: {p}")
    eng = _load_yaml_map(engine_yaml)
    srv = _load_yaml_map(serving_yaml)

    inst = eng.get("instances")
    if not isinstance(inst, dict):
        raise ValueError("ERR_CONFIG: subagent-engine.yaml: instances: block missing")  # noqa: TRY004
    for key in (
        "count",
        "port_base",
        "ctx_size",
        "max_num_seqs",
        "per_instance_budget_gb",
    ):
        if key not in inst:
            raise ValueError(
                f"ERR_CONFIG: subagent-engine.yaml: instances.{key} missing"
            )
    model = eng.get("model")
    if not isinstance(model, dict):
        raise ValueError(  # noqa: TRY004
            "ERR_CONFIG: subagent-engine.yaml: model: block missing"
        )
    # Byte sizes: uniform numeric edge (G3 PR #10 r14 P2) — int OR
    # positive float, bool excluded (same shape as
    # per_instance_budget_gb above), normalized to int at load. Bytes
    # are an integral quantity, so the int() normalization is exact for
    # whole-byte floats; a hand-edited `7.2e9` is still accepted and
    # normalized, matching the budget key's leniency.
    size_bytes = model.get("size_bytes")
    if not isinstance(size_bytes, (int, float)) or isinstance(size_bytes, bool):
        raise ValueError(  # noqa: TRY004
            "ERR_CONFIG: subagent-engine.yaml: model.size_bytes missing/not a number"
        )
    if size_bytes <= 0:
        raise ValueError(
            "ERR_CONFIG: subagent-engine.yaml: model.size_bytes not positive"
        )
    # Numeric instance keys: validate the TYPE at config-load (ERR_CONFIG)
    # instead of letting `int(cfg[...])` / `float(cfg[...])` in main()
    # raise a cryptic `ValueError: invalid literal for int()` mid-boot
    # (G3 PR #10 round 8 P2). A non-numeric string ("abc") reaches main()
    # only when the YAML is hand-edited — this is the early, named
    # failure. (Range checks are the S1.7 render-config tool's job;
    # the manager validates presence + type + sign.)
    for key in ("count", "port_base", "ctx_size", "max_num_seqs"):
        if not isinstance(inst.get(key), int) or isinstance(inst.get(key), bool):
            raise ValueError(  # noqa: TRY004  (ERR_CONFIG contract: ValueError, msg names it)
                f"ERR_CONFIG: subagent-engine.yaml: instances.{key} not an int"
            )
    if not isinstance(inst.get("per_instance_budget_gb"), (int, float)) or isinstance(
        inst.get("per_instance_budget_gb"), bool
    ):
        raise ValueError(  # noqa: TRY004
            "ERR_CONFIG: subagent-engine.yaml: instances.per_instance_budget_gb not a number"
        )
    alias = eng.get("alias")
    api_key = eng.get("api_key")
    if not isinstance(alias, str) or not isinstance(api_key, str):
        raise ValueError("ERR_CONFIG: subagent-engine.yaml: alias/api_key missing")  # noqa: TRY004
    # S1.6 `kv_cache` (S1.3 --cache-type-k/v): feeds the S1.4 footprint
    # (G3 PR #10 r13 P1). Only q4_0 has a LOCKED measured constant (SPEC
    # S1.4: 0.000003277, the MAP 1.7 re-anchor); an unvalidated edit to
    # e.g. f16 would silently gate VRAM on the q4_0 constant (the FP16
    # footprint is ~2.2x the q4_0 one — over-admission). A missing or
    # unknown dtype is an ERR_CONFIG at load, not a guess at boot.
    kv_cache = eng.get("kv_cache")
    if not isinstance(kv_cache, str):
        raise ValueError(  # noqa: TRY004
            "ERR_CONFIG: subagent-engine.yaml: kv_cache missing"
        )
    if kv_cache not in KV_PER_SLOT_PER_TOKEN_GB:
        raise ValueError(
            "ERR_CONFIG: subagent-engine.yaml: kv_cache "
            f"'{kv_cache}' has no locked S1.4 per_slot_per_token_GB "
            f"(locked: {sorted(KV_PER_SLOT_PER_TOKEN_GB)})"
        )
    # The S2.4 N=0 PTQ1_0 fallback quant (S1.1). NOT a hard requirement
    # of the MAP 2.1–2.5 skeleton — the fallback RETRY logic lands in
    # MAP 2.7 — but it is surfaced now so a malformed/missing fallback
    # quant is caught at config-load (ERR_CONFIG) or explicitly logged,
    # not discovered mid-N=0-handoff. Same uniform numeric edge as
    # size_bytes above (r14 P2).
    fallback_size_bytes = model.get("fallback_size_bytes")
    if fallback_size_bytes is not None and not (
        isinstance(fallback_size_bytes, (int, float))
        and not isinstance(fallback_size_bytes, bool)
        and fallback_size_bytes > 0
    ):
        raise ValueError(
            "ERR_CONFIG: subagent-engine.yaml: model.fallback_size_bytes not a positive number"
        )
    # S1.3 on-disk model filenames (MAP 2.7: the S2.4 fallback boot path
    # rewrites the unit's --model flag to model.fallback_file). Optional
    # here — a missing key just disables the surgical unit rewrite (the
    # caller logs it); the S1.6 schema validation of presence is
    # bonsai2_render_config.py's job (MAP 1.5).
    model_file = model.get("file")
    if model_file is not None and not isinstance(model_file, str):
        raise ValueError("ERR_CONFIG: subagent-engine.yaml: model.file not a string")
    fallback_model_file = model.get("fallback_file")
    if fallback_model_file is not None and not isinstance(fallback_model_file, str):
        raise ValueError(
            "ERR_CONFIG: subagent-engine.yaml: model.fallback_file not a string"
        )

    main_stack = srv.get("main_stack")
    if not isinstance(main_stack, dict):
        raise ValueError("ERR_CONFIG: serving.yaml: main_stack: block missing")  # noqa: TRY004
    if not isinstance(main_stack.get("container"), str):
        raise ValueError("ERR_CONFIG: serving.yaml: main_stack.container missing")  # noqa: TRY004
    if not isinstance(main_stack.get("endpoint"), str):
        raise ValueError("ERR_CONFIG: serving.yaml: main_stack.endpoint missing")  # noqa: TRY004
    footprint = main_stack.get("footprint_gb")
    if not isinstance(footprint, (int, float)):
        raise ValueError(  # noqa: TRY004
            "ERR_CONFIG: serving.yaml: main_stack.footprint_gb missing/not a number"
        )
    os_reserved = srv.get("os_reserved_gb")
    if not isinstance(os_reserved, (int, float)):
        raise ValueError(  # noqa: TRY004
            "ERR_CONFIG: serving.yaml: os_reserved_gb missing/not a number"
        )
    manager = srv.get("manager") or {}
    health_poll_s = manager.get("health_poll_s", 10)
    if not isinstance(health_poll_s, (int, float)) or health_poll_s <= 0:
        health_poll_s = 10
    boot_target_s = srv.get("boot_target_s", 180)
    if not isinstance(boot_target_s, (int, float)) or boot_target_s <= 0:
        boot_target_s = 180

    return {
        "engine": eng,
        "serving": srv,
        "alias": alias,
        "api_key": api_key,
        "weights_bytes": int(size_bytes),  # normalized (r14 P2)
        "weights_gb": size_bytes / 1e9,  # decimal GB (S1.4 convention)
        "fallback_size_bytes": (
            int(fallback_size_bytes) if fallback_size_bytes is not None else None
        ),  # normalized (r14 P2)
        "model_file": model_file,  # S1.3 on-disk name (MAP 2.7 unit rewrite)
        "fallback_model_file": fallback_model_file,
        "count": inst["count"],
        "port_base": inst["port_base"],
        "ctx_size": inst["ctx_size"],
        "slots": inst["max_num_seqs"],
        "kv_cache": kv_cache,
        "per_slot_per_token_gb": KV_PER_SLOT_PER_TOKEN_GB[kv_cache],
        "budget_gb": inst["per_instance_budget_gb"],
        "main_container": main_stack["container"],
        "main_endpoint": main_stack["endpoint"],
        "main_footprint_gb": float(footprint),
        "os_reserved_gb": float(os_reserved),
        "health_poll_s": float(health_poll_s),
        "boot_target_s": float(boot_target_s),
    }


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# free-memory reads
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
def _mem_available_gb() -> float:
    """The S1.4 tier's free-memory read on this GB10: /proc/meminfo
    MemAvailable in DECIMAL GB (the MAP 1.9 OOM test's convention —
    nvidia-smi --query-gpu memory fields are N/A on this unified-memory
    box; MemAvailable counts reclaimable page cache)."""
    for line in Path("/proc/meminfo").read_text().splitlines():
        if line.startswith("MemAvailable:"):
            return int(line.split()[1]) / 1e6  # kB -> decimal GB
    raise OSError("MemAvailable not in /proc/meminfo")


def _mem_available_gb_bounded() -> float:
    """_mem_available_gb with a REAL wall-clock cap (G3 PR #10 r14 P0).

    The S1.4 contract is '5 s timeout per probe' — a wedged probe must
    never deadlock the manager. /proc reads are normally instant, so a
    post-hoc deadline check would be nominal (the reviewer's point);
    the only stdlib way to genuinely bound a synchronous local read is
    a daemon thread + join(timeout): a wedged reader keeps spinning in
    the background (harmless — a pure file read, no resources of its
    own) while the caller gets its TimeoutError on time."""
    result: dict = {}

    def _run() -> None:
        try:
            result["v"] = _mem_available_gb()
        except BaseException as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 — re-raised below
            result["e"] = exc

    t = threading.Thread(target=_run, daemon=True)
    t.start()
    t.join(FREE_PROBE_TIMEOUT_S)
    if t.is_alive():
        raise TimeoutError(
            f"free-memory probe exceeded {FREE_PROBE_TIMEOUT_S:.0f}s (wedged /proc read)"
        )
    if "e" in result:
        raise result["e"]
    return result["v"]


def read_free_gb() -> float:
    """The S1.4 allocation-tier free read, bounded: one probe, one retry,
    then free = 0 (S1.4: "5 s timeout per probe, one retry; on a second
    timeout the tier treats free as 0"). The 5 s bound
    (FREE_PROBE_TIMEOUT_S) is ENFORCED per probe (r14 P0: a wedged
    reader raises TimeoutError at exactly the cap, inside the retry
    loop below); the load-bearing shape is retry-once-then-zero, which
    the hermetic test drives with a failing reader (the reader is
    injectable, so the thread-bounded real reader is exercised only
    live)."""
    for attempt in range(FREE_PROBE_RETRIES + 1):
        try:
            return _mem_available_gb_bounded()
        except (OSError, TimeoutError) as exc:
            _log(
                f"free-memory probe {attempt + 1} failed: {exc} "
                f"(retry={'yes' if attempt < FREE_PROBE_RETRIES else 'no -> free=0'})"
            )
    return 0.0


def read_total_gb() -> float:
    for line in Path("/proc/meminfo").read_text().splitlines():
        if line.startswith("MemTotal:"):
            return int(line.split()[1]) / 1e6
    raise OSError("MemTotal not in /proc/meminfo")


def _main_container_id(container: str) -> str | None:
    """docker container id (with the leading '/') for `container`, or None
    if the container does not exist (the S2.3 'live when the container
    exists' gate — else the footprint fallback)."""
    r = subprocess.run(
        ["docker", "inspect", "-f", "{{.Id}}", container],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        check=False,
    )
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None
    cid = r.stdout.strip()
    return cid or None


def main_used_gb(container: str, fallback_gb: float) -> tuple[float, str]:
    """S2.3: the main container's live cgroup `memory.current` (sleep-
    accurate — reflects the main stack's CURRENT pool usage, not the 76.4
    GB fresh-load default), else the recorded `main_stack.footprint_gb`
    fallback. Returns `(used_gb, source)` with source one of
    `"live cgroup"` / `"recorded footprint"` — the caller must log the
    SOURCE it actually used (not infer it from the value: a container
    genuinely sitting at exactly the recorded footprint would otherwise
    log the fallback path).

    Docker on this box (cgroup v2, systemd-managed) charges each container
    to /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/docker-<id>.scope/. We try the
    system.slice shape first, then the raw docker/ shape, so the read is
    robust across cgroup layouts. Any read failure (container gone,
    cgroup unreadable) falls back to the recorded footprint — the spec's
    'else the S2.6 main_stack.footprint_gb default'.
    """
    cid = _main_container_id(container)
    if cid:
        flat = cid.replace("/", "")
        for cand in (
            Path(f"/sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/docker-{flat}.scope/memory.current"),
            Path(f"/sys/fs/cgroup/docker/{flat}/memory.current"),
        ):
            if cand.is_file():
                try:
                    return int(cand.read_text().strip()) / 1e9, "live cgroup"
                except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
                    _log(
                        f"main cgroup read failed ({cand}): {exc} -> footprint fallback"
                    )
                    break
    return fallback_gb, "recorded footprint"


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# S2.3 boot-time budget check
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
def planning_tier(
    total_gb: float,
    main_used_gb: float,
    os_reserved_gb: float,
    n_yaml: int,
    budget_gb: float,
) -> tuple[int, float, list[str]]:
    """S2.3, one-to-one: `free_gb = total - main - reserved`;
    `N' = max(0, min(N, floor(free_gb / budget)))`. Returns
    (n_prime, free_gb, warnings). The `max(0, ...)` clamps a negative
    free (main + reserved already consume the pool) to main-only instead
    of a negative count (S2.3 verbatim; the S2.2 `max(0, ...)` note).

    Pure + total on its domain (the hermetic test drives every branch):
    budget <= 0 -> 0; free < 0 -> 0; 0 < N' < N -> a WARN_VRAM_SHORTFALL
    entry is appended (S2.3).
    """
    warnings: list[str] = []
    free_gb = total_gb - main_used_gb - os_reserved_gb
    if budget_gb <= 0:
        return 0, free_gb, [f"WARN_VRAM_SHORTFALL: budget {budget_gb} GB <= 0, N'=0"]
    n_prime = int(free_gb // budget_gb)
    n_prime = max(n_prime, 0)  # the S2.3 max(0, ...) clamp
    n_prime = min(n_yaml, n_prime)
    if 0 < n_prime < n_yaml:
        warnings.append(
            f"WARN_VRAM_SHORTFALL: N'={n_prime} < N={n_yaml} "
            f"(free {free_gb:.2f} GB / budget {budget_gb:.2f} GB)"
        )
    return n_prime, free_gb, warnings


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# S2.5 ctx reduction rule (shared by the S2.4 fallback boot path (MAP 2.7)
# and the S2.5 drift watch (MAP 2.8))
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
def halve_ctx(ctx: int) -> int:
    """S2.5 / S2.4 ctx reduction rule, verbatim: `halve (rounded down to
    a multiple of 8192, floor 8192)`.

    S2.5: `the manager rewrites that instance's --ctx-size to half
    (rounded down to a multiple of 8192, floor 8192)`. S2.4 fallback
    boot path: `the manager retries that one instance with the ctx
    halved (multiple of 8192, floor 8192 — the same reduction as the
    S2.5 drift rule)`. One pure function, one rule, both call sites:

      halve_ctx(262144) = 131072
      halve_ctx(131072) = 65536
      halve_ctx(8192)   = 8192   (the floor — never below)
      halve_ctx(8191)   = 8192   (sub-floor rounds up to the floor;
                                  ctx is always a multiple of 8192 by
                                  the S1.6/S2.5 invariants, so this
                                  case is defensive)
    """
    if ctx <= CTX_FLOOR:
        return CTX_FLOOR
    half = (ctx // 2) // CTX_HALVE_MULTIPLE * CTX_HALVE_MULTIPLE
    return max(half, CTX_FLOOR)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# S2.4 N=0 fallback (PTQ1_0 retry, MAP 2.7)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
def fallback_quant_available(cfg: dict) -> bool:
    """S2.4 (MAP 2.7): the PTQ1_0 retry is only possible when the config
    carries the fallback quant's size (model.fallback_size_bytes — the
    S1.1 ~5.95 GB PTQ1_0). Without it the N'=0 handoff stays a plain
    main-only (budget_zero); the S1.4 formula has no fallback weights
    to plan against."""
    return bool(cfg.get("fallback_size_bytes"))


def fallback_footprint_gb(cfg: dict) -> float:
    """S2.4: `recompute S1.4 with its footprint` — the PTQ1_0 weights +
    the UNCHANGED q4_0 KV at the same ctx/slots (S2.4 verbatim: 'the
    6.87 GB q4_0 KV at the same ctx/slots' — the KV term is dtype-
    pinned by `kv_cache`, quant-independent)."""
    return bonsai2_vram.instance_footprint_gb(
        cfg["fallback_size_bytes"] / 1e9,
        int(cfg["slots"]),
        int(cfg["ctx_size"]),
        cfg["per_slot_per_token_gb"],
    )


def fallback_budget_gb(cfg: dict) -> float:
    """S2.4: the PTQ1_0 per-instance BUDGET — the formula footprint +
    the S1.4 5% margin (`~12.82 GB formula footprint; +5% margin ->
    ~13.46 GB budget`, S2.4 verbatim). Unlike the allocation tier's
    gate term, this is NOT floored at the configured
    per_instance_budget_gb: the fallback exists precisely to admit an
    instance on a pool that could NOT fit the PQ2_0 cap (a max() floor
    at 14.78 GB would re-plan at the budget that just came out N'=0)."""
    return fallback_footprint_gb(cfg) * 1.05


def record_quant_in_use(engine_yaml: Path, quant: str, *, log=_log) -> bool:
    """S2.4 (MAP 2.7): `record the quant in use in subagent-engine.yaml`
    — a surgical, comment-preserving line rewrite: replace an existing
    `model.quant_in_use:` line, else insert one under the `model:`
    block. The key is informational for the pool state (which gguf the
    pool is serving); the units' --model flags are what actually run
    the quant (the fallback unit rewrites below), so the record is a
    state marker, not a boot input."""
    try:
        text = engine_yaml.read_text()
    except OSError as exc:
        log(f"record_quant_in_use: {engine_yaml}: {exc}")
        return False
    lines = text.splitlines(keepends=True)
    quant_line = (
        f"  quant_in_use: {quant}   # S2.4 N=0 fallback quant, "
        "recorded by the manager at boot (MAP 2.7)"
    )
    replaced = False
    for idx, line in enumerate(lines):
        if re.match(r"^  quant_in_use:", line):
            lines[idx] = quant_line + "\n"
            replaced = True
            break
    if not replaced:
        for idx, line in enumerate(lines):
            if re.match(r"^model:\s*$", line):
                lines.insert(idx + 1, quant_line + "\n")
                replaced = True
                break
    if not replaced:
        log(f"record_quant_in_use: no model: block in {engine_yaml}")
        return False
    tmp = engine_yaml.with_suffix(engine_yaml.suffix + ".tmp")
    tmp.write_text("".join(lines))
    os.replace(tmp, engine_yaml)
    log(f"S2.4: recorded quant_in_use={quant} in {engine_yaml.name}")
    return True


def record_ctx_in_use(engine_yaml: Path, ctx: int, *, log=_log) -> bool:
    """S2.4 (MAP 2.7): the S2.4 ctx-halve retry's bookkeeping —
    `The reduced ctx is recorded in subagent-engine.yaml
    (instances.ctx_size for that quant) so restarts stay consistent`:
    a surgical line rewrite of the `instances.ctx_size:` key (the same
    key the next boot reads for the footprint / the unit's --ctx-size),
    so a manager restart or install re-run keeps the reduced ctx
    instead of re-admitting the instance at the ctx that just OOM'd.
    Same shape as `record_quant_in_use` (replace-or-insert under the
    `instances:` block; insert only if the key line is missing — a
    missing key would be an ERR_CONFIG at load anyway, so this is
    defensive)."""
    try:
        text = engine_yaml.read_text()
    except OSError as exc:
        log(f"record_ctx_in_use: {engine_yaml}: {exc}")
        return False
    lines = text.splitlines(keepends=True)
    ctx_line = (
        f"  ctx_size: {int(ctx)}   # S2.4: reduced by the ctx-halve retry "
        "at boot (MAP 2.7); restarts stay consistent at this ctx"
    )
    for idx, line in enumerate(lines):
        # G3 PR#11 r1 P2: `\s?` (not `\s`) — a `ctx_size:262144` line
        # (valid YAML, no space after the colon) must be matched too,
        # or the replace path misses and the insert path appends a
        # DUPLICATE key under instances:.
        if re.match(r"^  ctx_size:\s?", line):
            lines[idx] = ctx_line + "\n"
            tmp = engine_yaml.with_suffix(engine_yaml.suffix + ".tmp")
            tmp.write_text("".join(lines))
            os.replace(tmp, engine_yaml)
            log(f"S2.4: recorded instances.ctx_size={int(ctx)} in {engine_yaml.name}")
            return True
    for idx, line in enumerate(lines):
        if re.match(r"^instances:\s*$", line):
            lines.insert(idx + 1, ctx_line + "\n")
            tmp = engine_yaml.with_suffix(engine_yaml.suffix + ".tmp")
            tmp.write_text("".join(lines))
            os.replace(tmp, engine_yaml)
            log(f"S2.4: recorded instances.ctx_size={int(ctx)} in {engine_yaml.name}")
            return True
    log(f"record_ctx_in_use: no instances: block in {engine_yaml}")
    return False


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# S1.4 allocation tier (the per-spawn stricter gate)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
def allocation_need_gb(
    weights_gb: float,
    slots: int,
    ctx_size: int,
    budget_gb: float,
    per_slot_per_token_gb: float = bonsai2_vram.PER_SLOT_PER_TOKEN_GB,
) -> float:
    """The S1.4 allocation-tier gate term:
    `max(instance_footprint_GB + 1.0, per_instance_budget_gb)`.

    The `+ 1.0` GB spike margin is S1.4's gate; the `max(..., budget)`
    floor is the S1.4-vs-S2.2 reconciliation (pin-reality delta
    2026-09-23): S2.2 caps each container at `per_instance_budget_gb`
    (= footprint + 5% margin), so on a box where the 5% margin > 1.0 GB
    the cap is the larger term and the tier must gate on the cap — a
    free pool between the two would pass `footprint + 1.0` but not fit
    the container. On this box the margin term is the max
    (need 15.08 > cap 14.78), so the floor is a no-op here; it binds on
    any box where budget > footprint + 1.0.

    `per_slot_per_token_gb` is the S1.4 KV constant for the configured
    `kv_cache` dtype (G3 PR #10 r13 P1: the S1.6 key now feeds the
    footprint — main() passes cfg['per_slot_per_token_gb']; the default
    is the module constant so pure-formula callers keep working).
    """
    return max(
        bonsai2_vram.instance_footprint_gb(
            weights_gb, slots, ctx_size, per_slot_per_token_gb
        )
        + TIER_MARGIN_GB,
        budget_gb,
    )


def allocation_tier(
    need_gb: float, free_reader, pre_charge_gb: float = 0.0
) -> tuple[bool, float, str]:
    """S1.4: `free >= allocation_need_gb` (the gate term is
    `max(footprint + 1.0, cap)` — see `allocation_need_gb`). `free_reader`
    is injectable (the hermetic test drives the bounded-read retry); the
    real reader is `read_free_gb`. Returns (granted, free_gb, detail).

    `pre_charge_gb` (G3 PR#11 r1 P1): bytes the caller has ALREADY
    committed from this same pool in the current pass — the S3.2 reload
    loop charges each in-pass start against the shared pool, the
    way `spawn_sequence`'s post-spawn re-read does at boot. A pass that
    re-reads a static pool for every target would over-grant on a tight
    pool (every target sees the pre-pass free value)."""
    free_gb = free_reader()
    available_gb = free_gb - pre_charge_gb
    granted = available_gb >= need_gb
    # Pinned log marker (G3 PR #10 r13 P2): granted means free >= need —
    # the printed inequality must be the GATE's, not the opposite.
    charge_note = (
        f" (after {pre_charge_gb:.2f} GB in-pass charge)" if pre_charge_gb else ""
    )
    detail = (
        f"S1.4 allocation tier: free {available_gb:.2f} GB "
        f"{'>=' if granted else '<'} need {need_gb:.2f} GB "
        f"(max(footprint + 1.0 GB margin, per-instance cap)){charge_note}"
    )
    return granted, free_gb, detail


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# instance health probe (S2.2) — the 'healthy' definition
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
def _probe_models(
    port: int, alias: str, api_key: str, timeout: float = 5.0, probe_timeout_s: float | None = None
) -> bool:
    """GET /v1/models -> 200 + the alias in the body (S2.2, half 1).

    `probe_timeout_s` (S2.8 #2 @gpu test hook, BONSAI2_PROBE_TIMEOUT_S):
    overrides the SOCKET cap for a dead/stuck port so a failed probe fails
    fast instead of occupying the cycle for the default 5 s. The default
    (None -> `timeout` = 5 s) is unchanged."""
    url = f"http://127.0.0.1:{port}/v1/models"
    req = urllib.request.Request(url, headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {api_key}"})
    cap = timeout if probe_timeout_s is None else probe_timeout_s
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=cap) as resp:
            if resp.status != 200:
                return False
            body = json.loads(resp.read())
        return alias in [m.get("id") for m in body.get("data", [])]
    except (urllib.error.URLError, json.JSONDecodeError, OSError, TimeoutError):
        return False


def _probe_completion(
    port: int,
    alias: str,
    api_key: str,
    max_tokens: int = COMPLETION_MAX_TOKENS,
    timeout: float = COMPLETION_RT_MAX_S + 5.0,
    probe_timeout_s: float | None = None,
) -> bool:
    """A `max_tokens` completion round-trips in < COMPLETION_RT_MAX_S
    (S2.2, half 2 — 'catches alive-but-stuck'). Under
    --reasoning-format deepseek a trivial prompt may land entirely in
    reasoning_content (content: '') — the acceptance is the ROUND-TRIP,
    not the text (the MAP 1.7 finding); any populated message is alive.

    The `timeout` kwarg (COMPLETION_RT_MAX_S + 5 s, G3 PR #10 r13 P2
    clarification) is the urlopen SOCKET cap, not the acceptance bar:
    it only bounds how long a stuck connect may occupy the probe; the
    S2.2 '< 10 s' gate is the measured `elapsed` (timer starts before
    urlopen — connect + send + read are all counted), checked against
    COMPLETION_RT_MAX_S. A 14 s round-trip therefore gets a response,
    then fails the gate on elapsed — exactly the alive-but-stuck case
    half 2 exists to catch."""
    url = f"http://127.0.0.1:{port}/v1/chat/completions"
    body = json.dumps(
        {
            "model": alias,
            "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Say hello in one word."}],
            "max_tokens": max_tokens,
            "stream": False,
        }
    ).encode()
    req = urllib.request.Request(
        url,
        data=body,
        headers={
            "Authorization": f"Bearer {api_key}",
            "Content-Type": "application/json",
        },
        method="POST",
    )
    t0 = time.monotonic()
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as resp:
            parsed = json.loads(resp.read())
    except (urllib.error.URLError, json.JSONDecodeError, OSError, TimeoutError):
        return False
    elapsed = time.monotonic() - t0
    if elapsed >= COMPLETION_RT_MAX_S:
        return False
    try:
        msg = parsed["choices"][0].get("message", {})
    except (KeyError, IndexError, TypeError):
        return False
    return bool(msg.get("content") or msg.get("reasoning_content"))


def instance_healthy(
    port: int, alias: str, api_key: str, probe_timeout_s: float | None = None
) -> bool:
    """S2.2 health probe: /v1/models 200+alias AND a 16-token completion
    RT < 10 s. Both halves required (a models-200 that can't complete is
    alive-but-stuck). `probe_timeout_s` (the S2.8 #2 @gpu test hook,
    BONSAI2_PROBE_TIMEOUT_S) caps the probe's socket timeout so a dead
    probe fails fast instead of occupying the cycle; the S2.2 '< 10 s'
    healthy gate (COMPLETION_RT_MAX_S) is unchanged."""
    return _probe_models(
        port, alias, api_key, probe_timeout_s=probe_timeout_s
    ) and _probe_completion(
        port, alias, api_key, probe_timeout_s=probe_timeout_s
    )


def main_healthy(endpoint: str, timeout: float = 5.0) -> bool:
    """The main endpoint is UP (liveness probe, S2.1). `GET /v1/models`
    on the main stack returns 401 (up, demanding the main's api key) or
    200 — both mean 'the engine is answering'. Semantics: 2xx = up; 401 =
    up (auth-walled, the expected healthy shape — the manager does not
    hold the main stack's key); 404/410 = DOWN (the route was moved or
    removed — a moved /v1/models is NOT liveness: the manager's
    health.json `main_healthy` feeds the harness exit-82 gate, SPEC
    S3.1, and S3.1 treats the main-endpoint probe as a 2xx liveness
    contract — G3 PR #10 r11 P1; earlier any-4xx-as-up was a 'route
    moved' hedge that over-reported a genuinely gone route as up);
    other 4xx (405 etc.) = up (a route-level error — the server
    answered, so the route exists and the engine is answering); 5xx,
    connection refused, timeout, non-HTTP error = down.

    Accepted limitation (G3 PR #10 r12 P1, note for later): the probe
    sends no Authorization header, so a dead-but-listening proxy that
    answers 401 is indistinguishable from a healthy auth-walled main —
    the harness exit-82 gate (S3.1/S3.6 `main_endpoint_healthy_probe`)
    will not fire on auth-only breakage. Accepted: S2.1 says the main
    stack is independent infrastructure (the pool's probes carry the key;
    the manager's liveness probe deliberately does not), and a dead main
    that still answers 401 is a main-stack problem, not a pool one. A
    keyed probe is a MAP 2.6 candidate if the exit-82 gate ever proves
    to under-fire.

    Timeout: a
    single hard 5 s urlopen cap (no separate read/connect split) —
    noted in the docstring only; the probe-loop blocking cost of a
    black-holed main is accepted in the skeleton (G3 PR #10 r5 P2,
    revisit if MAP 2.6 adds concurrent probes)."""
    req = urllib.request.Request(endpoint.rstrip("/") + "/v1/models", method="GET")
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as resp:
            resp.read(64)
        return True
    except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:
        # 2xx handled above. 404/410 = route moved/removed = down
        # (r11 P1); 401 + other 4xx = up (the server answered).
        return (200 <= e.code < 500) and e.code not in (404, 410)
    except (urllib.error.URLError, OSError, TimeoutError):
        return False


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# systemctl spawn (S2.5)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
def systemctl_base() -> list[str]:
    """The systemctl prefix. System-scope in production (the S2.2 units
    are system units); user-scope under the @gpu test harness when
    BONSAI2_USER_SYSTEMCTL=1 (non-root; user@1000 is active on this box)."""
    if os.environ.get("BONSAI2_USER_SYSTEMCTL") == "1":
        return ["systemctl", "--user"]
    return ["systemctl"]


def systemctl_start_instance(i: int, base: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    """`systemctl start bonsai2-server@i` (S2.5). Returns the exit code."""
    cmd = (base or systemctl_base()) + ["start", f"bonsai2-server@{i}"]
    r = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        _log(
            f"systemctl start bonsai2-server@{i} rc={r.returncode}: {r.stderr.strip()[-300:]}"
        )
    return r.returncode


def systemctl_state(i: int, base: list[str] | None = None) -> dict:
    """`systemctl show -p SubState -p Result -p NRestarts` for the unit.
    Returns the RAW systemd values (e.g. SubState="exited"/"running",
    Result="success"), NOT S3.1 state strings — the SubState/Result ->
    S3.1 mapping is applied by the consumers (`_refresh_states`,
    `check_escalation`).

    Consumed by the S2.2 escalation (MAP 2.6): `NRestarts` detects a
    unit that systemd itself restarted since the fail streak began
    (`Restart=on-failure` is the unit's own policy; the manager's
    five-fail restart is a BACKSTOP on top of it, and must not
    double-restart a unit systemd already brought back — the restart
    then resets the unit's start-limit counter, S2.2, and the streak
    defers to the fresh load).
    """
    unit = f"bonsai2-server@{i}"
    r = subprocess.run(
        (base or systemctl_base())
        + ["show", "-p", "SubState", "-p", "Result", "-p", "NRestarts", unit],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        check=False,
    )
    state = {}
    for line in r.stdout.splitlines():
        if "=" in line:
            k, _, v = line.partition("=")
            state[k.strip()] = v.strip()
    return state


def systemctl_restart_instance(i: int, base: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    """`systemctl restart bonsai2-server@i` (S2.2 five-fail restart,
    MAP 2.6; S2.5 drift in-place restart, MAP 2.8). `restart` (unlike
    `start`) resets the unit's start-limit counter, so neither path
    can trip `bfailed` by itself (S2.2/S2.5). Returns the exit code."""
    cmd = (base or systemctl_base()) + ["restart", f"bonsai2-server@{i}"]
    r = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        _log(f"systemctl restart bonsai2-server@{i} rc={r.returncode}: {r.stderr.strip()[-300:]}")
    return r.returncode


def systemctl_stop_instance(i: int, base: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    """`systemctl stop bonsai2-server@i` (S3.2 unload, MAP 2.10 —
    `systemctl stop` -> the unit's ExecStartPre `docker rm -f`, the
    same signal path as `cancel`; S2.5: an explicit unload beats the
    drift watch, so a `bunloaded` instance is never re-spawned).
    Returns the exit code."""
    cmd = (base or systemctl_base()) + ["stop", f"bonsai2-server@{i}"]
    r = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        _log(f"systemctl stop bonsai2-server@{i} rc={r.returncode}: {r.stderr.strip()[-300:]}")
    return r.returncode


def instance_cgroup_path(name: str) -> Path | None:
    """The instance container's cgroup dir (S2.5 drift watch, MAP 2.8).

    `name` is the docker container name (`bonsai2-server-i` — the S2.2
    `--name` flag). Docker on this box (cgroup v2, systemd-managed)
    charges each container to `/sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/
    docker-<id>.scope/` (probed 2026-09-22, the S2.3 main-cgroup read);
    the raw `docker/<id>/` shape is tried second (cross-layout robust,
    same convention as `main_used_gb`). None when the container is
    gone (the unit stopped / drift-restarted before the read).
    """
    r = subprocess.run(
        ["docker", "inspect", "-f", "{{.Id}}", name],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        check=False,
    )
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None
    cid = r.stdout.strip()
    if not cid:
        return None
    flat = cid.replace("/", "")
    for cand in (
        Path(f"/sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/docker-{flat}.scope"),
        Path(f"/sys/fs/cgroup/docker/{flat}"),
    ):
        if cand.is_dir():
            return cand
    return None


def cgroup_memory_current(cgroup_dir: Path | None) -> int | None:
    """`memory.current` (bytes) in a cgroup v2 dir; None on any read
    failure (the caller treats an unreadable cgroup as 'no pressure
    signal this cycle' — the S2.5 `> 30 s over the cap` rule cannot
    fire from a missing reading)."""
    if cgroup_dir is None:
        return None
    try:
        return int((cgroup_dir / "memory.current").read_text().strip())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None


def rewrite_unit_ctx(
    unit_path: Path, new_ctx: int, current_ctx: int, *, log=_log
) -> bool:
    """S2.5 drift watch (MAP 2.8): rewrite the instance unit's rendered
    `--ctx-size` from `current_ctx` to `new_ctx` IN PLACE (same unit
    name, same port, same image — the S2.5 'container-level restart,
    not systemd re-plumbing' contract).

    The units are installed by install.sh (S6.3) from the S2.2
    template; at install time each concrete unit carries the rendered
    `--ctx-size {ctx}` in its ExecStart. The rewrite is surgical:
    replace the first `--ctx-size <current_ctx> ` occurrence with
    `--ctx-size <new_ctx> ` — no re-render from the template (the
    template is a placeholder install.sh owns, not the runtime unit),
    no other line touched. Returns True iff the rewrite landed
    (False = no occurrence found — the caller logs + defers; the
    restart would then run with the old ctx, which is the unit's own
    business, not a drift-watch failure).
    """
    if not unit_path.is_file():
        log(f"drift watch: unit {unit_path} missing — deferring ctx rewrite")
        return False
    text = unit_path.read_text()
    marker = f"--ctx-size {current_ctx} "
    if marker not in text:
        log(
            f"drift watch: unit {unit_path} has no '--ctx-size {current_ctx} ' "
            f"— deferring (the unit's ExecStart drifted from the recorded ctx)"
        )
        return False
    text = text.replace(marker, f"--ctx-size {new_ctx} ", 1)
    tmp = unit_path.with_suffix(unit_path.suffix + ".tmp")
    tmp.write_text(text)
    os.replace(tmp, unit_path)
    log(
        f"drift watch: {unit_path.name}: --ctx-size {current_ctx} -> {new_ctx} "
        f"(S2.5 halve, multiple of {CTX_HALVE_MULTIPLE}, floor {CTX_FLOOR})"
    )
    return True


def rewrite_unit_model(unit_path: Path, model_file: str, *, log=_log) -> bool:
    """S2.4 fallback boot path (MAP 2.7): rewrite the instance unit's
    `--model` flag to the PTQ1_0 gguf IN PLACE (same unit name, same
    port, same image — the image bakes BOTH quants at
    /opt/bonsai2/models/, S1.3/S6.1). Surgical: replace the first
    `--model /opt/bonsai2/models/<anything> ` occurrence; False when
    the unit has no --model flag (the caller logs + proceeds — the
    unit then runs its default quant, which on a fresh install is the
    configured one)."""
    if not unit_path.is_file():
        log(f"fallback boot: unit {unit_path} missing — deferring model rewrite")
        return False
    text = unit_path.read_text()
    m = re.search(r"--model /opt/bonsai2/models/\S+ ", text)
    if not m:
        log(
            f"fallback boot: unit {unit_path.name} has no "
            "'--model /opt/bonsai2/models/...' flag — deferring "
            "(the unit's ExecStart drifted from the S1.3 shape)"
        )
        return False
    text = text.replace(m.group(0), f"--model /opt/bonsai2/models/{model_file} ", 1)
    tmp = unit_path.with_suffix(unit_path.suffix + ".tmp")
    tmp.write_text(text)
    os.replace(tmp, unit_path)
    log(
        f"fallback boot: {unit_path.name}: --model -> "
        f"/opt/bonsai2/models/{model_file} (S2.4 PTQ1_0 retry)"
    )
    return True


def rewrite_unit_memory(unit_path: Path, old_budget_gb: float, new_budget_gb: float, *, log=_log) -> bool:
    """S2.4 fallback boot path (MAP 2.7): rewrite the instance unit's
    `--memory` / `--memory-swap` flags from the PQ2_0 budget to the
    PTQ1_0 budget (the S2.2 `--memory` = the per-instance budget, so
    the fallback instance must carry the FALLBACK budget — the S1.4
    5%-margin figure for the PTQ1_0 footprint; otherwise the container
    cap would still be the 14.78 GB PQ2_0 cap on a ~13.46 GB fallback
    load). Same surgical shape as `rewrite_unit_model`: both flags,
    first occurrence each, the `g` suffix kept verbatim (the S2.2
    main-stack pattern; the docker GiB parse pin-reality delta applies
    equally to both values).

    G3 PR#11 r1 P1: the OLD budget is read from the unit file via
    regex, not reconstructed from the config float — `str(float)`
    rendering ("14.8" vs a unit rendered "14.80") would make the
    exact-occurrence check miss and the fallback silently boot under
    the PQ2_0 cap. The NEW value is written at 2 decimals: the S2.2
    rendered-unit precision (install.sh's template is `%.2f`), which
    is also the precision the @gpu assertions and the docker `g` parse
    expect (int(X * 2^30) truncation makes the 2-decimal form the
    stable, comparable one)."""
    if not unit_path.is_file():
        log(f"fallback boot: unit {unit_path} missing — deferring memory rewrite")
        return False
    text = unit_path.read_text()
    m_mem = re.search(r"--memory (\d+(?:\.\d+)?)g ", text)
    m_swap = re.search(r"--memory-swap (\d+(?:\.\d+)?)g ", text)
    if not m_mem or not m_swap:
        log(
            f"fallback boot: unit {unit_path.name} has no "
            "'--memory <budget>g' / '--memory-swap <budget>g' flags — "
            "deferring (the unit's ExecStart drifted from the S2.2 shape)"
        )
        return False
    old_mem = m_mem.group(1)
    old_swap = m_swap.group(1)
    new_mem = f"{new_budget_gb:.2f}"
    new_swap = f"{new_budget_gb:.2f}"
    text = text.replace(f"--memory {old_mem}g ", f"--memory {new_mem}g ", 1).replace(
        f"--memory-swap {old_swap}g ", f"--memory-swap {new_swap}g ", 1
    )
    tmp = unit_path.with_suffix(unit_path.suffix + ".tmp")
    tmp.write_text(text)
    os.replace(tmp, unit_path)
    log(
        f"fallback boot: {unit_path.name}: --memory/--memory-swap "
        f"{old_mem}g -> {new_mem}g (S2.4 PTQ1_0 budget)"
    )
    return True




def wait_instance_healthy(
    i: int, cfg: dict, boot_deadline_s: float | None = None
) -> bool:
    """Wait for instance i to reach the S2.2 'healthy' definition, polling
    every HEALTH_PROBE_POLL_S. Returns True iff it went healthy in time.

    Deadline: `boot_deadline_s` (an absolute monotonic() deadline) when
    given — the SHARED S2.8 #1 whole-boot budget (S2.5: 'waits each to
    healthy before the next'; S2.8 #1: 'Cold boot → main + N healthy ≤
    180 s (recorded T)'). A per-instance `boot_target_s` deadline would
    let N instances each burn the full budget (N × 180 s for N=8 =
    1440 s, far beyond S2.8 #1's 180 s whole-boot acceptance — G3 PR #10
    r11 P2); the shared deadline caps the WHOLE spawn sequence at
    boot_target_s. When None (direct call outside the spawn sequence)
    the per-instance `boot_target_s` deadline applies.

    Polling is the health probe itself (models + completion), not just a
    unit-state poll: a unit that is 'active' but whose engine is
    alive-but-stuck is not 'healthy' by S2.2. (No systemctl base param —
    the wait is a port probe, not a unit-state poll; G3 PR #10 r6 P2.)"""
    port = cfg["port_base"] + i
    if boot_deadline_s is None:
        deadline = time.monotonic() + cfg["boot_target_s"]
    else:
        deadline = boot_deadline_s
    last = False
    while time.monotonic() < deadline:
        last = instance_healthy(port, cfg["alias"], cfg["api_key"])
        if last:
            return True
        time.sleep(HEALTH_PROBE_POLL_S)
    return last

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# S3.4 reaper pass (manager-side deadline enforcement, MAP 3.4)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
_harness_mod = None


def _load_harness_module():
    """Load the shared harness core (same bin/ dir, the MAP 0.1 1:1
    deploy layout) for the reaper pass. Loaded lazily once (the manager
    is a long-lived daemon; the harness module's imports are stdlib +
    PyYAML, the same edge the manager already takes)."""
    global _harness_mod
    if _harness_mod is None:
        import importlib.machinery
        import importlib.util

        bin_dir = Path(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
        loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(
            "bonsai2_harness_reaper", str(bin_dir / "bonsai2_harness.py")
        )
        spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader("bonsai2_harness_reaper", loader)
        if spec is None or spec.loader is None:
            raise ImportError("harness core spec unavailable")
        mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
        spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
        _harness_mod = mod
    return _harness_mod


def _results_dirs_from_harness_cfg() -> Path | None:
    """The S3.4 reaper's task-record dir, by the harness command's own
    convention: `BONSAI2_RESULTS_DIR` overrides, else `results_dir`
    from the harness.yaml (`BONSAI2_CONFIG` overrides its path, default
    /opt/bonsai2/etc/harness.yaml). A missing/unreadable config is the
    DEFAULT results dir — the reaper must not die on config drift (the
    commands' exit-2 rule does not apply to a daemon pass)."""
    results_dir = os.environ.get("BONSAI2_RESULTS_DIR", "").strip()
    if not results_dir:
        try:
            cfg_path = Path(
                os.environ.get("BONSAI2_CONFIG", "/opt/bonsai2/etc/harness.yaml")
            )
            data = _load_yaml_map(cfg_path)
            val = data.get("results_dir")
            if isinstance(val, str) and val:
                results_dir = val
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            pass
    if not results_dir:
        results_dir = "/run/bonsai2/results"
    base = Path(results_dir)
    return base if (base / "tasks").is_dir() else None


def reap_tasks_once(health_dir: Path, log=_log) -> list[str]:
    """One manager-side S3.4 reaper pass (runs every health cycle,
    after the control pass): reaps stale `running`/`engine_retried`
    task records (dead worker pid -> failed/ERR_ENGINE_DOWN; the
    deadline backstop past the grace -> timeout/ERR_DEADLINE), freeing
    the slot so the claim queue forward-progresses. The task records
    live in the harness results dir (this function's convention); a
    missing dir = nothing to reap (hermetic boots with no tasks).
    Returns the reaped task_ids. The pass is fault-tolerant at the
    caller (a reaper error must never kill the steady loop)."""
    base = _results_dirs_from_harness_cfg()
    if base is None:
        return []
    d = {"base": base, "tasks": base / "tasks", "locks": base / ".locks"}
    h = _load_harness_module()
    return h.reap_stale(d, log=log)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# S2.2 escalation + S2.5 drift + S3.2 control (steady-loop state machine)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
class _InstanceState:
    """Per-instance steady-loop bookkeeping (the MAP 2.6/2.8/2.10
    state machine). One per configured index (i in 0..count-1);
    alignment is BY INDEX, not by list position — the boot skeleton
    sized its state list to the spawned prefix, the steady loop owns
    the full configured set.

      state: S3.1 instance state string
      fails: consecutive S2.2 probe fails (resets on a healthy probe)
      restarted_at: monotonic() of the last unit-level restart observed
        (manager-issued OR a systemd-side NRestarts increment) — the
        five-fail escalation defers to a fresh load instead of
        double-restarting (the restart resets the unit's start-limit
        counter, S2.2)
      ctx: the instance's LIVE ctx (halved by the drift watch / the
        S2.4 ctx-halve retry; the config ctx is the boot-time value,
        this is what the unit currently runs — S3.1 `ctx` is the
        live value)
      ctx_reduced: True once any halving has happened (S3.1/S2.5)
      pressure_start: monotonic() when the cgroup first read over the
        S2.2 --memory cap this cycle (the S2.5 `> 30 s sustained`
        clock); None when at/below cap
      restarts: manager-issued unit restarts (drift + escalation) —
        the S2.5 'counted in that instance's restart budget' book
        (global acceptance #5: <= 2 per instance over the 72 h soak)
    """

    __slots__ = (
        "i",
        "state",
        "fails",
        "restarted_at",
        "last_nrestarts",
        "ctx",
        "ctx_reduced",
        "pressure_start",
        "restarts",
    )

    def __init__(self, i: int, ctx: int, state: str = "bdown"):
        self.i = i
        self.state = state
        self.fails = 0
        self.restarted_at: float | None = None
        # -1 = NRestarts not yet observed; the S2.2 escalation (MAP 2.6)
        # detects a systemd-side restart in between probe passes by a
        # MONOTONIC NRestarts increment (it can never decrease), so a
        # stale (low) baseline can only ever trigger the defer branch,
        # which is the safe one.
        self.last_nrestarts = -1
        self.ctx = int(ctx)
        self.ctx_reduced = False
        self.pressure_start: float | None = None
        self.restarts = 0


class Runtime:
    """The manager's steady-loop runtime state (one instance entry per
    configured index; `spawned` = the indexes with a unit started).
    Replaces the skeleton's `spawned: list[int]` + `states: list[str]`
    pair, which only sized the spawned prefix — the S3.2 control path
    (MAP 2.10) and the S2.5 drift watch (MAP 2.8) both need per-index
    bookkeeping for the FULL configured set (a `bunloaded` index is
    one that was granted at boot but stopped by the user; the drift
    watch must see it and not re-spawn it)."""

    def __init__(self, cfg: dict, spawned: list[int], boot_states: list[str]):
        count = int(cfg["count"])
        ctx0 = int(cfg["ctx_size"])
        entries = [_InstanceState(i, ctx0, "bdown") for i in range(count)]
        for idx, i in enumerate(spawned):
            st = boot_states[idx] if idx < len(boot_states) else "bdown"
            if i < count:
                entries[i].state = st
        self.cfg = cfg
        self.entries: list[_InstanceState] = entries
        self.spawned: list[int] = list(spawned)
        # main_only_reason is only PRESENT when instances: 0 (S2.4);
        # the value ("user_unloaded" vs "budget_zero") is caller-set.
        self.main_only_reason: str | None = None

    def entry(self, i: int) -> _InstanceState | None:
        return self.entries[i] if 0 <= i < len(self.entries) else None

    @property
    def running(self) -> list[int]:
        return [
            i for i in self.spawned if self.entries[i].state != "bunloaded"
        ]

    @property
    def running_states(self) -> list[str]:
        return [self.entries[i].state for i in self.running]

    def states_for_health(self) -> list[str]:
        """The S3.1 `instances` list: the RUNNING set (a `bunloaded`
        index is dropped from the document — S3.1: 'Note: bunloaded =
        the user explicitly unloaded that instance; it is not a
        failure state, routing skips it' — it is not an instance
        running)."""
        return [self.entries[i].state for i in self.running]


def initial_states(spawned: list[int], start_rc: dict[int, int], cfg: dict) -> list[str]:
    """The first health.json's states (the skeleton's build, kept as a
    function so the boot path and the tests share it): a unit whose
    `systemctl start` returned non-zero reflects its S1.5 bfailed path
    DIRECTLY in health.json; a clean start that has not gone healthy
    yet is `degraded` (alive-but-unproven), not bfailed. (MAP 2.6
    replaces the steady-loop miss-mapping, not this boot-time build —
    the boot-time build probes exactly once per index.)"""
    states = []
    for i in spawned:
        if start_rc.get(i, 0) != 0:
            states.append("bfailed")
        else:
            port = _port(cfg, i)
            states.append(
                "healthy"
                if instance_healthy(port, cfg["alias"], cfg["api_key"])
                else "degraded"
            )
    return states


def _instance_unit_path(i: int, sysctl_base: list[str]) -> Path:
    """The instance unit's path (system or user scope, following
    `sysctl_base` — the same scope split as `systemctl_base`)."""
    if sysctl_base and sysctl_base[-1] == "--user":
        return Path.home() / ".config" / "systemd" / "user" / f"bonsai2-server@{i}.service"
    return Path("/etc/systemd/system") / f"bonsai2-server@{i}.service"


def _refresh_states(
    runtime: Runtime, spawned: list[int], log=_log, probe_timeout_s: float | None = None
) -> None:
    """Re-probe each RUNNING instance; map to an S3.1 state (S2.2,
    MAP 2.6).

      * healthy probe -> healthy; the fail counter RESETS (the S2.2
        two-consecutive rule only counts CONSECUTIVE fails).
      * miss, fails+1 == 1 -> keep the prior state (a single transient
        miss is NOT degraded yet — the skeleton's first-miss-over-report
        is replaced, G3 PR #10 r9 P2: 'MAP 2.6 replaces this with the
        two-fail counter').
      * miss, fails+1 >= DEGRADED_FAILS (2) -> degraded (the S2.2
        'two consecutive fails -> degraded' rule, verbatim).
      * a `bfailed`/`degraded`/`bdown` instance keeps its state on a
        miss (only a fresh healthy probe clears it — unchanged from
        the skeleton).
    `spawned` is the CURRENT running set (indexes aligned to
    runtime.entries BY INDEX — the parameter is the caller's snapshot
    of `runtime.running` taken this cycle, so a control path that
    mutates `runtime` mid-cycle cannot desync the two)."""
    for i in spawned:
        e = runtime.entry(i)
        if e is None:
            continue
        port = _port(runtime.cfg, i)
        if probe_timeout_s is None:
            _ok = instance_healthy(port, runtime.cfg["alias"], runtime.cfg["api_key"])
        else:
            _ok = instance_healthy(
                port,
                runtime.cfg["alias"],
                runtime.cfg["api_key"],
                probe_timeout_s=probe_timeout_s,
            )
        if _ok:
            e.fails = 0
            e.state = "healthy"
        else:
            e.fails += 1
            if e.state == "healthy" or e.state == "degraded":
                if e.fails >= DEGRADED_FAILS:
                    e.state = "degraded"
            # bfailed / bdown: preserved on a miss (skeleton rule)


def check_escalation(
    runtime: Runtime,
    now: float,
    *,
    base: list[str] | None = None,
    unit_show=subprocess.run,
    restart=systemctl_restart_instance,
    log=_log,
) -> None:
    """S2.2 five-fail escalation (MAP 2.6): `five consecutive fails ->
    restart attempt`, applied per RUNNING instance.

    Semantics:
      * fails >= RESTART_FAILS (5) AND the unit has NOT restarted on
        its own since the streak's start (a systemd-side NRestarts
        increment since `restarted_at` would mean the unit's own
        Restart=on-failure already acted — the manager defers, no
        double restart): issue `systemctl restart bonsai2-server@i`
        (the restart resets the unit's start-limit counter, S2.2, and
        the instance counts it in its restart budget — S2.5 'counted
        in that instance's restart budget', global #5).
      * a healthy probe already reset `fails`, so a recovering
        instance never reaches the streak.
      * the state stays `degraded` across the restart (the fresh load
        is unproven; the next healthy probe clears it to `healthy`).
    `now` is injectable (hermetic tests advance a fake clock);
    `unit_show`/`restart` are injectable (hermetic tests fake the
    systemctl surface)."""
    base = base if base is not None else systemctl_base()

    def _nrestarts(i: int) -> int:
        show = unit_show(
            base
            + ["show", "-p", "NRestarts", f"bonsai2-server@{i}"],
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            check=False,
        )
        for line in show.stdout.splitlines():
            if line.startswith("NRestarts="):
                try:
                    return int(line.partition("=")[2].strip())
                except ValueError:
                    return -1
        return -1

    for i in runtime.running:
        e = runtime.entry(i)
        if e is None:
            continue
        if e.fails < RESTART_FAILS:
            # G3 PR#11 r1 P1: the NRestarts baseline is captured ONCE, at
            # the streak start (the 0 -> 1 transition) — not re-read and
            # re-advanced on every probe cycle. Advancing it while
            # fails < RESTART_FAILS would silently absorb a
            # systemd-side restart that lands mid-streak, and the
            # defer check at the threshold would compare against the
            # POST-restart value, missing it. Reading systemctl only on
            # the transition (and at the threshold below) also drops
            # the N-1 subprocess calls per probe cycle the old shape
            # made (the r1 cost note).
            if e.fails == 1 and e.last_nrestarts < 0:
                e.last_nrestarts = _nrestarts(i)
            continue
        n_restarts = _nrestarts(i)
        # -1 = the baseline was never observed (e.g. the streak started
        # before this manager process saw the unit) — no systemd-side
        # restart can be DETECTED from it, so the manager's restart is
        # not deferred on an unknown baseline (a stale -1 can only mean
        # 'unknown', never 'restarted since the streak began').
        if e.last_nrestarts >= 0 and n_restarts > e.last_nrestarts:
            # The unit restarted on ITS OWN since the streak began
            # (systemd's Restart=on-failure already acted — the restart
            # reset the unit's start-limit counter, S2.2, and the fail
            # clock is now measuring a FRESH load): defer, no double
            # restart.
            log(
                f"escalation: i={i} unit already restarted since the "
                f"fail streak began (NRestarts {e.last_nrestarts} -> "
                f"{n_restarts}) — deferring to the fresh load"
            )
            e.fails = 0
            e.state = "degraded"  # unproven until the next healthy probe
            continue
        rc = restart(i, base)
        e.restarts += 1
        e.restarted_at = now
        e.last_nrestarts = n_restarts + 1  # the restart bumped it (systemd)
        e.fails = 0
        e.state = "degraded"  # unproven until the next healthy probe
        log(
            f"S2.2 escalation: i={i} {RESTART_FAILS} consecutive probe "
            f"fails -> systemctl restart bonsai2-server@{i} (rc={rc}, "
            f"manager restarts={e.restarts})"
        )


def check_drift(
    runtime: Runtime,
    now: float,
    cap_bytes: int,
    *,
    base: list[str] | None = None,
    cgroup_path=instance_cgroup_path,
    cgroup_read=cgroup_memory_current,
    rewrite=rewrite_unit_ctx,
    restart=systemctl_restart_instance,
    log=_log,
) -> None:
    """S2.5 drift watch (MAP 2.8), one recheck: for each RUNNING
    instance, read its cgroup `memory.current` against the S2.2
    `--memory` cap.

      * over cap: start/continue the pressure clock — sustained over
        cap for > DRIFT_PRESSURE_S (30 s, S2.5 verbatim) -> halve the
        instance's ctx (S2.5 rule: `halve, rounded down to a multiple
        of 8192, floor 8192`), rewrite the unit's `--ctx-size` in
        place, `systemctl restart bonsai2-server@i` in place (same
        unit name/port/image; the restart resets the start-limit
        counter so the drift handling itself cannot trip bfailed,
        S2.5), record `ctx_reduced: true` + the new ctx (S3.1), count
        it in the instance's restart budget.
      * at/below cap: the clock resets (the `> 30 s sustained` rule
        needs the over-cap condition CONTINUOUS).
      * `bunloaded` instances are NOT in `runtime.running` — never
        re-spawned (S2.5/S3.2: an explicit unload beats the drift
        watch).
      * ctx-grow is never done (v1 is conservative, S2.5).
    `now` + the cgroup/rewrite/restart surfaces are injectable (the
    hermetic tests drive the 30 s clock without wall time).
    """
    base = base if base is not None else systemctl_base()
    for i in runtime.running:
        e = runtime.entry(i)
        if e is None:
            continue
        cur = cgroup_read(cgroup_path(f"bonsai2-server-{i}"))
        if cur is None:
            # unreadable (container gone this cycle — e.g. mid-restart):
            # keep the clock running (a missing reading is not a
            # recovery reading); the next cycle re-reads.
            continue
        if cur > cap_bytes:
            if e.pressure_start is None:
                e.pressure_start = now
            elif (now - e.pressure_start) > DRIFT_PRESSURE_S:
                new_ctx = halve_ctx(e.ctx)
                # G3 PR#11 r1 P2: at the ctx floor the halve is a
                # no-op — skip the in-place restart entirely instead of
                # burning a restart-budget count on a restart that
                # changes nothing (the clock still resets below).
                if new_ctx == e.ctx:
                    e.pressure_start = None
                    log(
                        f"S2.5 drift: i={i} cgroup {cur} B > cap "
                        f"{cap_bytes} B sustained > {DRIFT_PRESSURE_S:.0f} s, "
                        f"but ctx is already at the floor {CTX_FLOOR} — "
                        f"no ctx change, no restart (clock reset)"
                    )
                    continue
                unit = _instance_unit_path(i, base)
                if rewrite(unit, new_ctx, e.ctx, log=log):
                    e.ctx = new_ctx
                    e.ctx_reduced = True
                    rc = restart(i, base)
                    e.restarts += 1
                    e.restarted_at = now
                    e.fails = 0
                    e.pressure_start = None
                    e.state = "degraded"  # unproven until re-healthy
                    log(
                        f"S2.5 drift: i={i} cgroup {cur} B > cap {cap_bytes} B "
                        f"sustained > {DRIFT_PRESSURE_S:.0f} s -> ctx "
                        f"{new_ctx} (halved, floor {CTX_FLOOR}), "
                        f"systemctl restart in place (rc={rc}, "
                        f"manager restarts={e.restarts}, ctx_reduced=true)"
                    )
        else:
            e.pressure_start = None


def _port_free_check(port: int, timeout: float = 1.0) -> bool:
    """True when 127.0.0.1:port is NOT accepting connections (the
    unload drain's 'the engine is gone' check — a refused connect is
    'gone'; a live engine answering 401 on /v1/models is still up)."""
    import socket as _socket

    s = _socket.socket(_socket.AF_INET, _socket.SOCK_STREAM)
    s.settimeout(timeout)
    try:
        s.connect(("127.0.0.1", port))
        return False  # something accepted — still up
    except OSError:
        return True  # refused / no listener — gone
    finally:
        s.close()


def wait_port_down(
    port: int, timeout_s: float = 5.0, poll_s: float = 0.5, check=_port_free_check
) -> bool:
    """Wait up to `timeout_s` for 127.0.0.1:port to stop accepting
    (the S3.2 unload's 'instant VRAM release' verification — the unit
    stop + `docker rm -f` takes a bounded moment; the manager polls,
    and reports the stop's own rc even on a late-down port rather than
    blocking the cycle indefinitely). G3 PR#11 r1 P2: the default is 5 s
    (not 30 s) — the stop is `docker rm -f`, which is near-instant; 5 s
    is ample, and 30 s x N would stall the 10 s probe cadence (the
    health.json write, escalation, and drift all sit behind this call)."""
    deadline = time.monotonic() + timeout_s
    while True:
        if check(port):
            return True
        if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            return False
        time.sleep(poll_s)


def process_control(
    control_dir: Path,
    runtime: Runtime,
    now: float,
    *,
    base: list[str] | None = None,
    free_reader=read_free_gb,
    start=systemctl_start_instance,
    stop=systemctl_stop_instance,
    wait_healthy=wait_instance_healthy,
    wait_down=wait_port_down,
    log=_log,
) -> None:
    """S3.2 unload/reload manager-level semantics (MAP 2.10), one pass
    over the `control/` request queue (the Phase-3 `bonsai2-unload` /
    `bonsai2-reload` commands write one JSON request file each; the
    manager is the sole pool actor — S3.2 'Both are manager-mediated').

    Request shape (the command owns the write; the manager owns the
    ack): `{"type": "unload"|"reload", "instances": [i, ...] | "all",
    "request_id": "<uuid-ish>"}`.

    UNLOAD (S3.2): per target index (in ascending order) —
      * already `bunloaded`/not-running -> no-op for that index
        (idempotent, S3.2: 'unload of an already-unloaded ... instance
        is a no-op for that index');
      * running -> `systemctl stop bonsai2-server@i` (the unit's
        ExecStartPre `docker rm -f`, the same signal path as
        `cancel`), wait for the port to go down (bounded), mark
        `bunloaded` in health.json (S2.4: 'with zero running
        instances the stack enters main-only mode with
        main_only_reason: user_unloaded'). `--all` = every configured
        index not already unloaded.
    RELOAD (S3.2): per target index — the normal path: S1.4 allocation
    tier (the CURRENT config need), S2.2 unit start, S2.2 health
    re-probe (healthy <= boot_target_s). A tier fail at a target ->
    that index stays `bunloaded`, the others proceed, the ack carries
    `"state": "partial"` + a WARN_VRAM_SHORTFALL detail (S3.2: 'a
    partial reload ... is still a valid state — the S1.4 i > 0
    partial-load rule, not the i = 0 exit-4 case').
    ACK: `control/ack-<request_id>.json` = `{"type": ...,
    "reloaded"/"unloaded": [i...], "state": "ok"|"partial",
    "ts": <epoch>}`; the request file is renamed `.done` on success.
    A dead manager writes no ack — the Phase-3 command's exit-84
    path (S3.2: 'a dead manager -> exit 84')."""
    if not control_dir.is_dir():
        return
    for req_file in sorted(control_dir.iterdir()):
        if req_file.suffix != ".json" or not req_file.is_file():
            continue
        try:
            req = json.loads(req_file.read_text())
        except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError) as exc:
            log(f"control: unreadable request {req_file.name}: {exc} — leaving in place")
            continue
        rtype = req.get("type")
        targets = req.get("instances")
        req_id = str(req.get("request_id") or req_file.stem)
        ack: dict = {"type": rtype, "request_id": req_id, "ts": now}
        # G3 PR#11 r1 P0: explicit validation — the old mixed
        # `or ... and ...` expression was fragile: a list target like
        # ["all"] passed it, then crashed on int("all") AFTER the ack
        # was decided (no error ack, request left in place, re-errored
        # every control pass). Valid targets: the STRING "all", or a
        # list of ints (bools excluded — type(t) is int, not
        # isinstance).
        if rtype not in ("unload", "reload") or not (
            targets == "all"
            or isinstance(targets, list) and all(type(t) is int for t in targets)
        ):
            ack.update(
                state="error",
                detail="bad request: type must be unload|reload; instances must be a list of int indexes or 'all'",
            )
            _write_ack(control_dir, req_file, ack)
            continue
        idxs = [i for i in range(int(runtime.cfg["count"]))] if targets == "all" else list(targets)
        idxs = [i for i in idxs if 0 <= i < len(runtime.entries)]
        if rtype == "unload":
            done = []
            for i in sorted(idxs):
                e = runtime.entries[i]
                if e.state == "bunloaded":
                    continue  # idempotent no-op (S3.2)
                # G3 PR#11 r1 P0: only an index the manager actually
                # manages gets stopped — an index that was never spawned
                # (or whose unit already died, bdown/bfailed with the
                # container gone) is a no-op for that index, not a
                # systemctl stop + a state change that the next reload
                # would have to explain through the allocation tier again.
                if i not in runtime.spawned:
                    log(f"S3.2 unload: i={i} not spawned — no-op (S3.2)")
                    continue
                rc = stop(i, base)
                if rc != 0:
                    # G3 PR#11 r1 P1: a failed stop leaves the container
                    # possibly running — keep the prior state (the probe
                    # loop re-observes it) instead of believing a stop
                    # that failed; the index stays in spawned so the next
                    # control pass can retry the stop.
                    log(
                        f"S3.2 unload: i={i} systemctl stop rc={rc} — "
                        f"keeping state={e.state} (the stop did not land)"
                    )
                    continue
                down = wait_down(_port(runtime.cfg, i))
                e.state = "bunloaded"
                e.fails = 0
                e.pressure_start = None
                done.append(i)
                log(f"S3.2 unload: i={i} stopped, port-down={down}")
            runtime.spawned = [
                i for i in runtime.spawned if runtime.entries[i].state != "bunloaded"
            ]
            # S2.4: zero running -> main-only user_unloaded (the value the
            # next health write carries while the set stays empty).
            if not runtime.spawned:
                runtime.main_only_reason = "user_unloaded"
            ack.update(unloaded=sorted(done), state="ok")
        else:  # reload
            need_gb = _allocation_need_from_cfg(runtime.cfg)
            reloaded: list[int] = []
            partial = False
            # G3 PR#11 r1 P1: the S1.4 tier gates on the SHARED pool —
            # charge each in-pass start against it (the spawn_sequence
            # equivalent: the post-start re-read sees the pool with the
            # new instance's footprint gone). A static pool read per
            # target would over-grant on a tight pool with N > 1.
            in_pass_charge_gb = 0.0
            for i in sorted(idxs):
                e = runtime.entries[i]
                if e.state != "bunloaded":
                    continue  # idempotent: only unloaded indexes reload
                granted, free_gb, detail = allocation_tier(
                    need_gb, free_reader, in_pass_charge_gb
                )
                if not granted:
                    log(f"reload i={i}: WARN_VRAM_SHORTFALL {detail} — stays bunloaded")
                    partial = True
                    continue
                rc = start(i, base)
                if i not in runtime.spawned:
                    runtime.spawned.append(i)
                    runtime.spawned.sort()
                if rc != 0:
                    e.state = "bfailed"
                    log(f"reload i={i}: systemctl start rc={rc} -> bfailed")
                    continue
                ok = wait_healthy(i, runtime.cfg, None)
                e.state = "healthy" if ok else "degraded"
                e.fails = 0
                e.pressure_start = None
                if ok:
                    reloaded.append(i)
                    in_pass_charge_gb += need_gb
                else:
                    partial = True
                log(f"reload i={i}: healthy={ok} ({detail})")
            if runtime.spawned:
                runtime.main_only_reason = None
            ack.update(
                reloaded=sorted(reloaded),
                state="partial" if partial else "ok",
                detail="WARN_VRAM_SHORTFALL" if partial else None,
            )
        _write_ack(control_dir, req_file, ack)
        log(f"control: {rtype} {idxs} -> {ack.get('state')} (ack {req_id})")


def _write_ack(control_dir: Path, req_file: Path, ack: dict) -> None:
    """Write the ack file + mark the request done (S3.2 manager-mediated
    ack — the Phase-3 command polls for `ack-<request_id>.json`)."""
    req_id = ack.get("request_id") or req_file.stem
    ack_file = control_dir / f"ack-{req_id}.json"
    tmp = ack_file.with_suffix(".tmp")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(ack, indent=2) + "\n")
    os.replace(tmp, ack_file)
    try:
        os.replace(req_file, req_file.with_suffix(".json.done"))
    except OSError as exc:  # already consumed / raced — the ack is written
        _log(f"control: could not mark {req_file.name} done: {exc}")


def _allocation_need_from_cfg(cfg: dict) -> float:
    """The S1.4 gate term for the CURRENT config (reload path, S3.2):
    `max(footprint + 1.0, cap)` — same derivation as main() (the
    reload runs the allocation tier with the config the manager
    booted with, S3.2 're-spawns the targeted indexes through the
    normal path')."""
    footprint = bonsai2_vram.instance_footprint_gb(
        cfg["weights_gb"],
        int(cfg["slots"]),
        int(cfg["ctx_size"]),
        cfg["per_slot_per_token_gb"],
    )
    return max(footprint + TIER_MARGIN_GB, float(cfg["budget_gb"]))


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# S3.1 health.json


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
def _port(cfg: dict, i: int) -> int:
    return int(cfg["port_base"]) + i


def _instance_entry(
    cfg: dict, i: int, state: str, ctx: int | None = None, ctx_reduced: bool = False
) -> dict:
    slots = int(cfg["slots"])
    return {
        "i": i,
        "port": _port(cfg, i),
        "state": state,
        # S3.1: the instance's LIVE ctx (the S2.5 drift watch / S2.4
        # ctx-halve retry halve it; the config value is the boot-time
        # default) + ctx_reduced (S2.5 'recorded in health.json as
        # ctx_reduced: true + the new ctx value').
        "ctx": int(cfg["ctx_size"]) if ctx is None else int(ctx),
        "ctx_reduced": bool(ctx_reduced),
        "slots_in_use": 0,  # the harness owns live task slots (MAP P3)
        "slots_total": slots,
    }


def stack_summary(instance_states: list[str]) -> str:
    """Derive the S3.1 `stack` from the instance states.

    S3.1: `stack` in {healthy, degraded, main-only}; `main_only_reason`
    present ONLY when instances: 0 (S2.4) — the reason is the caller's
    (budget_zero / user_unloaded / ERR_VRAM_OOM), not derived here.
    Zero instances -> main-only; any bdown/bfailed -> degraded; any
    degraded -> degraded; else healthy. (Returned as a bare str — the
    main-only reason is the caller's to supply; the former tuple second
    was always None, G3 PR #10 r12 P1.)"""
    if not instance_states:
        return "main-only"
    if any(s in ("bdown", "bfailed") for s in instance_states):
        return "degraded"
    if any(s == "degraded" for s in instance_states):
        return "degraded"
    return "healthy"


def build_health(
    cfg: dict,
    states: list[str],
    main_ok: bool,
    main_only_reason: str | None = None,
) -> dict:
    """The S3.1 schema, one document. `states` is a list of S3.1 instance
    states aligned to the RUNNING set (empty = main-only). The boot-time
    path (MAP 2.1–2.5) builds docs from the spawn outcome; the steady
    loop builds them via `build_runtime_health` (which carries each
    instance's LIVE ctx + ctx_reduced, S2.5/S3.1)."""
    stack = stack_summary(states)
    doc: dict = {
        "stack": stack,
        "instances": [_instance_entry(cfg, i, st) for i, st in enumerate(states)],
        "main_endpoint": cfg["main_endpoint"],
        "main_healthy": bool(main_ok),
    }
    # S2.4: main_only_reason present only when instances: 0 — echo the
    # caller's cause (budget_zero / user_unloaded / ERR_VRAM_OOM); the
    # stack summary never supplies it for >=1 instance (the S3.1
    # 'reason present when instances: 0' contract — G3 PR #10 r12 P1
    # removed a vestigial `elif reason:` branch that could never fire).
    if not states:
        doc["main_only_reason"] = main_only_reason or "budget_zero"
    return doc


def build_runtime_health(cfg: dict, runtime: Runtime, main_ok: bool) -> dict:
    """The S3.1 document from the steady-loop Runtime (MAP 2.6/2.8/2.10):
    the RUNNING set's states, each instance's LIVE ctx + ctx_reduced
    (S2.5/S3.1: `ctx_reduced: true` + the new `ctx` value), and the
    runtime's main_only_reason (present only while the running set is
    empty — the S3.1 contract)."""
    running = runtime.running
    states = [runtime.entries[i].state for i in running]
    doc: dict = {
        "stack": stack_summary(states),
        "instances": [
            _instance_entry(
                cfg,
                i,
                runtime.entries[i].state,
                ctx=runtime.entries[i].ctx,
                ctx_reduced=runtime.entries[i].ctx_reduced,
            )
            for i in running
        ],
        "main_endpoint": cfg["main_endpoint"],
        "main_healthy": bool(main_ok),
    }
    if not running and runtime.main_only_reason is not None:
        doc["main_only_reason"] = runtime.main_only_reason
    return doc


def write_health(health_dir: Path, doc: dict) -> None:
    """Atomic write of health.json (tmp + rename) so a reader never sees a
    partial file. Creates the (tmpfs) dir if missing — the install-time
    unit is responsible for it, but the manager must not die if it is
    absent on a cold start (S2.2 'emits health JSON on /run/bonsai2')."""
    health_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    final = health_dir / "health.json"
    tmp = health_dir / "health.json.tmp"
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(doc, indent=2) + "\n")
    os.replace(tmp, final)


def write_boot_target_s(serving_yaml: Path, boot_target_s: float, *, log=_log) -> bool:
    """S2.6 (MAP 2.9): `boot_target_s` is MEASURED, not guessed — written
    back to serving.yaml from the measured boot (the S2.7 reboot test's
    T). A surgical, comment-preserving line rewrite of the top-level
    `boot_target_s:` key: replace an existing occurrence in place, else
    append it as the final top-level key (the tracked template keeps it
    last, S2.6). The value is rounded to a whole second (S2.6's example
    is `180`, an int) with a minimum of 10 s (a measurement below the
    health-poll cadence is noise, not a target). Returns True on a
    write; False (log, no file change) when the file is missing."""
    target = max(10, int(round(float(boot_target_s))))
    new_line = (
        f"boot_target_s: {target}   # measured, not guessed; "
        "updated by install test (S2.7) / the manager's measured boot (MAP 2.9)"
    )
    try:
        text = serving_yaml.read_text()
    except OSError as exc:
        log(f"write_boot_target_s: {serving_yaml}: {exc} (skipped)")
        return False
    lines = text.splitlines(keepends=True)
    replaced = False
    for idx, line in enumerate(lines):
        if re.match(r"^boot_target_s:\s", line):
            lines[idx] = new_line + "\n"
            replaced = True
            break
    if not replaced:
        lines.append(new_line + "\n")
    tmp = serving_yaml.with_suffix(serving_yaml.suffix + ".tmp")
    tmp.write_text("".join(lines))
    os.replace(tmp, serving_yaml)
    log(f"S2.6: measured boot {boot_target_s:.1f} s -> boot_target_s={target} "
        f"written to {serving_yaml.name}")
    return True


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# spawn sequence (S2.5) — the ordered grant-and-wait loop
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
def spawn_sequence(
    cfg: dict,
    n_prime: int,
    need_gb: float,
    *,
    free_reader=read_free_gb,
    systemctl_start=systemctl_start_instance,
    wait_healthy=wait_instance_healthy,
    log=_log,
    sysctl_base: list[str] | None = None,
    boot_deadline_s: float | None = None,
) -> tuple[int, list[int], str, dict[int, int]]:
    """Spawn the granted instances IN ORDER, running the S1.4 allocation
    tier before each (S2.5 + S1.4 + S2.3). Returns (exit_code,
    spawned_indexes, detail, start_rc) where `start_rc` maps each spawned
    index to its `systemctl start` rc (0 = the start issued cleanly).

    `boot_deadline_s` (an absolute monotonic() deadline, set by the
    caller) is the SHARED S2.8 #1 whole-boot budget passed to
    `wait_healthy` — None leaves `wait_healthy` to its own per-instance
    deadline (the hermetic tests' fake waits take `(i, cfg)` and do not
    see this kwarg).

    The S1.4 two-outcome allocation tier drives the exit code:
      * fail at i > 0  -> WARN_VRAM_SHORTFALL, stop at i-1, exit 0
        (the 'i > 0 partial load, exit 0' rule — a valid state)
      * fail at i = 0  -> exit 4 ERR_VRAM_OOM (granted at plan time,
        short at allocation time; S1.4 'the manager exits 4 before the
        instance's KV allocation')
    n_prime = 0 -> no loop body -> exit 0 (the caller handles the S2.4
    main-only handoff / MAP 2.7 PTQ1_0 fallback).

    Every dependency is injectable (free_reader, systemctl_start,
    wait_healthy) so the hermetic test proves the ORDERING (i=0 must reach
    healthy before i=1's systemctl start is issued) and the partial/exit-4
    branches without a GPU.
    """
    spawned: list[int] = []
    start_rc: dict[int, int] = {}
    for i in range(n_prime):
        granted, free_gb, detail = allocation_tier(need_gb, free_reader)
        log(f"i={i} {detail}")
        if not granted:
            if i == 0:
                return EXIT_VRAM_OOM, spawned, f"ERR_VRAM_OOM: {detail}", start_rc
            log(
                f"WARN_VRAM_SHORTFALL {{i: {i}, free: {free_gb:.2f}, need: {need_gb:.2f}}} "
                f"— partial load, proceeding with {len(spawned)} instance(s)"
            )
            return EXIT_OK, spawned, f"partial load at i={i}: {detail}", start_rc
        rc = systemctl_start(i, sysctl_base)
        start_rc[i] = rc
        spawned.append(i)
        if rc != 0:
            # A unit that fails to START (docker missing, port collision the
            # unit can't clear) is not a VRAM condition — S1.5 routes it to
            # the unit's bfailed path. Record it as a spawned index (the
            # start_rc surfaces the failure to the caller for health.json)
            # but keep going: later instances are independent (S2.1). Skip
            # the healthy wait: polling a dead engine's port for up to
            # boot_target_s is wasted time (the S2.5 "wait each to healthy
            # before the next" guarantee still holds — the NEXT start is
            # gated on this index's bookkeeping, not a healthy probe).
            # MAP 2.6's escalation restarts the bfailed unit.
            log(
                f"i={i} systemctl start rc={rc} (recorded as bfailed, "
                f"skipping healthy wait, continuing to next)"
            )
            continue
        ok = wait_healthy(i, cfg, boot_deadline_s)
        log(f"i={i} healthy={ok} (S2.5: wait before next)")
        # S2.5: 'waits each to healthy BEFORE the next' — the wait happens
        # inline. A not-yet-healthy instance is still spawned (the probe
        # loop / MAP 2.6 will restart it per the S2.2 restart policy); the
        # ordering guarantee is that the NEXT systemctl start is gated on
        # this one's wait, which it is by construction. Skeleton scope: a
        # slow load that exceeds boot_target_s is left 'degraded' by the
        # first health.json and re-probed every health_poll_s; the restart
        # for one that never recovers is MAP 2.6's (the S2.2 five-fail
        # escalation), not the skeleton's (G3 PR #10 r9 P2).
    return (
        EXIT_OK,
        spawned,
        f"full load: {len(spawned)} instance(s) spawned",
        start_rc,
    )


def fallback_retry(
    cfg: dict,
    engine_yaml: Path,
    sysctl_base: list[str],
    boot_deadline_s: float,
    *,
    spawn=spawn_sequence,
    log=_log,
) -> tuple[int, list[int], str, dict[int, int], int]:
    """S2.4 fallback boot path (MAP 2.7): when the S2.3 planning tier
    yielded N'=0 at the PQ2_0 budget, `install retries once with the
    PTQ1_0 fallback (S1.1, ~18% smaller)` — re-plan at the PTQ1_0
    budget, re-write instance 0's unit to the PTQ1_0 quant + the
    fallback budget, re-run the S1.4 allocation tier + the S2.5 spawn
    for that one instance.

    The S2.4 ctx-halve retry: `if the allocation tier still fails at
    the default --ctx-size ... the manager retries that one instance
    with the ctx halved (multiple of 8192, floor 8192 — the same
    reduction as the S2.5 drift rule) and re-runs the allocation tier
    on the reduced footprint; only if that also fails does the S1.4
    ERR_VRAM_OOM exit 4 apply.`

    The reduced ctx is recorded in `subagent-engine.yaml`
    (`instances.ctx_size` for that quant) so restarts stay consistent;
    `record_quant_in_use` marks the quant in use (both are surgical,
    comment-preserving rewrites of the installed file — the caller
    passes the DEPLOY path, which under the @gpu harness is the
    tracked template, so tests copy it first).

    Returns (exit_code, spawned, detail, start_rc, ctx_in_use):
      * 0 + [0] — the fallback instance spawned (possibly at the
        halved ctx); ctx_in_use = the ctx the unit now runs.
      * EXIT_VRAM_OOM + [] — the tier defeated the fallback instance
        at BOTH the default and the halved ctx (the S1.4 exit-4 case,
        main-only is NOT the outcome — S2.4 'Live-margin OOM').
    `spawn` is injectable (the hermetic tests fake the whole spawn;
    the real path runs it for n_prime=1)."""
    fb_budget = fallback_budget_gb(cfg)
    fb_need = max(
        fallback_footprint_gb(cfg) + TIER_MARGIN_GB,
        fb_budget,
    )
    fb_cfg = dict(cfg)
    fb_cfg["budget_gb"] = fb_budget
    fb_cfg["weights_gb"] = cfg["fallback_size_bytes"] / 1e9
    record_quant_in_use(engine_yaml, "PTQ1_0", log=log)
    _log(
        f"S2.4: N'=0 at the PQ2_0 budget -> PTQ1_0 retry: budget "
        f"{fb_budget:.2f} GB (footprint {fallback_footprint_gb(cfg):.2f} + 5%), "
        f"need {fb_need:.2f} GB"
    )
    unit0 = _instance_unit_path(0, sysctl_base)
    if cfg.get("fallback_model_file"):
        rewrite_unit_model(unit0, cfg["fallback_model_file"], log=log)
    # cfg["budget_gb"] is the normalized per_instance_budget_gb (PQ2_0
    # cap in the installed unit; load_config normalizes the key).
    rewrite_unit_memory(unit0, float(cfg["budget_gb"]), fb_budget, log=log)

    exit_code, spawned, detail, start_rc = spawn(
        fb_cfg, 1, fb_need, log=log, sysctl_base=sysctl_base, boot_deadline_s=boot_deadline_s
    )
    ctx_in_use = int(cfg["ctx_size"])
    if exit_code == EXIT_OK and spawned:
        _log(f"S2.4: PTQ1_0 fallback instance 0 spawned ({detail})")
        return EXIT_OK, spawned, f"PTQ1_0 fallback: {detail}", start_rc, ctx_in_use

    # The allocation tier defeated the fallback instance at the default
    # ctx (S1.4's exit-4 at i = 0). The S2.4 ctx-halve retry.
    new_ctx = halve_ctx(ctx_in_use)
    _log(
        f"S2.4: allocation tier failed at the default ctx "
        f"({detail}) -> ctx-halve retry: ctx {ctx_in_use} -> {new_ctx} "
        f"(multiple of {CTX_HALVE_MULTIPLE}, floor {CTX_FLOOR})"
    )
    rewrite_unit_ctx(
        unit0, new_ctx, ctx_in_use, log=lambda msg: _log(f"{msg} (ctx-halve retry)")
    )
    ctx2_cfg = dict(fb_cfg)
    ctx2_cfg["ctx_size"] = new_ctx
    need2 = max(
        bonsai2_vram.instance_footprint_gb(
            cfg["fallback_size_bytes"] / 1e9,
            int(cfg["slots"]),
            new_ctx,
            cfg["per_slot_per_token_gb"],
        )
        + TIER_MARGIN_GB,
        fb_budget,
    )
    exit_code2, spawned2, detail2, start_rc2 = spawn(
        ctx2_cfg,
        1,
        need2,
        log=log,
        sysctl_base=sysctl_base,
        boot_deadline_s=boot_deadline_s,
    )
    if exit_code2 == EXIT_OK and spawned2:
        ctx_in_use = new_ctx
        # S2.4: 'The reduced ctx is recorded in subagent-engine.yaml
        # (instances.ctx_size for that quant) so restarts stay
        # consistent' — the unit's --ctx-size was already rewritten
        # (rewrite_unit_ctx above); this records the YAML key too.
        record_ctx_in_use(engine_yaml, new_ctx, log=log)
        _log(f"S2.4: PTQ1_0 fallback instance 0 spawned at the halved ctx ({detail2})")
        return (
            EXIT_OK,
            spawned2,
            f"PTQ1_0 fallback + ctx-halve: {detail2}",
            start_rc2,
            ctx_in_use,
        )
    _log(
        f"S2.4: the ctx-halve retry also failed ({detail2}) -> "
        "ERR_VRAM_OOM exit 4 (S1.4 live-margin OOM, S2.4)"
    )
    return (
        EXIT_VRAM_OOM,
        [],
        f"ERR_VRAM_OOM (PTQ1_0 + ctx-halve): {detail2}",
        {},
        ctx_in_use,
    )


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# steady probe loop (S2.2; MAP 2.6 owns the degraded->restart escalation)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
def _drift_cap_bytes(budget_gb: float) -> int:
    """The S2.5 drift watch's per-instance cap in BYTES: the S2.2
    `--memory` flag's value. S2.2 caps the container at
    `per_instance_budget_gb` decimal GB; the kernel enforces that
    decimal value in bytes (the docker GiB parse only affects the
    flag string, not the cgroup limit the kernel sees — the S2.2
    pin-reality delta is about the FLAG, the S2.5 watch compares the
    cgroup against the BUDGET, decimal-GB end to end, S1.4 convention).
    `int(budget_gb * 1e9)`: a cgroup exactly AT the cap is not
    'over' — the S2.5 rule is strictly `> cap` sustained."""
    return int(float(budget_gb) * 1e9)


def run_probe_loop(
    cfg: dict,
    runtime: Runtime,
    health_dir: Path,
    settle_s: float,
    *,
    drift_recheck_s: float | None = None,
    drift_pressure_s: float | None = None,
    log=_log,
) -> int:
    """The steady state (S2.2 health probe + S2.2 escalation + S2.5
    drift watch + S3.2 control, MAP 2.6/2.8/2.10): every
    `health_poll_s`, re-probe main + each running instance (S2.2),
    apply the two-fail->degraded / five-fail->restart escalation
    (S2.2), process the `control/` request queue (S3.2), atomically
    rewrite health.json (S3.1 schema, with live ctx + ctx_reduced).

    The S2.5 drift recheck runs on its OWN 5-minute cadence
    (`drift_recheck_s`, default 300 s, S2.5: 're-checks every 5 min')
    — NOT every probe cycle: the 30 s pressure window is tracked
    across rechecks (the clock lives on the instance entry, so a
    recheck that sees the cgroup back under cap resets it; the
    between-recheck probe cycles only track probe fails).

    Runs forever under systemd (settle_s == 0); exits 0 after settle_s
    when non-zero (the @gpu test harness + a manual run). At least one
    full cycle always runs before the settle check (a settle run is a
    normal ready boot — the first health.json is written, G3 PR #10
    r5 P1's READY ordering is main()'s job, the loop's contract is the
    document).

    The probe cadence itself is overridable via the documented test
    env hook `BONSAI2_HEALTH_POLL_S` (falls back to the
    serving.yaml `health_poll_s` value when unset or not a positive
    number) — the same pattern as `BONSAI2_DRIFT_RECHECK_S` /
    `BONSAI2_DRIFT_PRESSURE_S` below. `BONSAI2_PROBE_TIMEOUT_S` (same
    pattern, docs/two-red-gpu-tests-fix.md) additionally caps the probe's
    socket timeout so a dead probe fails fast. The S2.8 #2 @gpu test
    (technical debt from the PR #11 r1 disposition) uses the 2 s cadence +
    a ~3 s probe cap: degraded = 2 consecutive fails, each failing in ~3 s
    on a dead port, so the witness lands in ~6-8 s, well inside the unit's
    RestartSec=5 + warm-reload down window. No port-holder machinery.
    """
    raw = os.environ.get("BONSAI2_HEALTH_POLL_S", "").strip()
    try:
        health_poll_s = float(raw) if raw else float(cfg["health_poll_s"])
    except ValueError:
        health_poll_s = float(cfg["health_poll_s"])
    if health_poll_s <= 0:
        health_poll_s = float(cfg["health_poll_s"])
    if drift_recheck_s is None:
        drift_recheck_s = float(
            os.environ.get("BONSAI2_DRIFT_RECHECK_S", DRIFT_RECHECK_S)
        )
    if drift_pressure_s is None:
        drift_pressure_s = float(
            os.environ.get("BONSAI2_DRIFT_PRESSURE_S", DRIFT_PRESSURE_S)
        )
    # S2.8 #2 @gpu test hook (docs/two-red-gpu-tests-fix.md): cap the
    # probe's socket timeout so a dead engine's failed probe fails FAST
    # instead of occupying the cycle for the default 15 s (5 s models +
    # 15 s completion round-trip). The S2.2 'two consecutive fails ->
    # degraded' rule then registers inside the ~8-20 s down window the
    # unit's Restart=on-failure + RestartSec=5 leaves, so the S2.8 #2
    # @gpu test can witness degraded without port-holder machinery. The
    # S2.2 '< 10 s' healthy gate (COMPLETION_RT_MAX_S) is unchanged —
    # this only bounds how long a stuck connect may occupy a cycle.
    # Same env-hook pattern as the three above; unset / non-positive ->
    # None (the probe's own default socket cap).
    probe_timeout_s: float | None = None
    raw_pt = os.environ.get("BONSAI2_PROBE_TIMEOUT_S", "").strip()
    if raw_pt:
        try:
            val = float(raw_pt)
            if val > 0:
                probe_timeout_s = val
        except ValueError:
            probe_timeout_s = None
    # G3 PR#11 r1 P2: the interval is the recheck period itself (the
    # `>=` gate tolerates the small overshoot); the old `- 1.0` shrank
    # the cadence 1 s per cycle, so the 5-minute recheck crept earlier
    # and earlier over a long run.
    drift_interval_s = max(drift_recheck_s, 1.0)
    cap_bytes = _drift_cap_bytes(float(cfg["budget_gb"]))
    base = systemctl_base()
    last_drift_at = -float("inf")
    cycle = 0
    t0 = time.monotonic()
    while True:
        now = time.monotonic()
        main_ok = main_healthy(cfg["main_endpoint"])
        # Snapshot the running set BEFORE the control pass: a control
        # that stops instances mid-cycle must not desync the probe
        # pass from the runtime (the entries are indexed by i, so the
        # snapshot is the set as of cycle start).
        running_snapshot = list(runtime.running)
        _refresh_states(
            runtime, running_snapshot, log, probe_timeout_s=probe_timeout_s
        )
        check_escalation(runtime, now, base=base, log=log)
        control_dir = health_dir / "control"
        try:
            process_control(control_dir, runtime, now, base=base, log=log)
        except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 — the control pass must
            # never kill the steady loop (a bad request file is the
            # command's fault, not the manager's; it stays in place,
            # the ack is the command's error signal).
            log(f"control pass error (cycle {cycle}): {exc!r}")
        # S3.4 (MAP 3.4): the manager-side deadline enforcement — one
        # reaper pass per cycle (stale running records -> terminal +
        # slot freed). A reaper error never kills the steady loop.
        try:
            reaped = reap_tasks_once(health_dir, log=log)
            if reaped:
                log(f"S3.4 reaper: {len(reaped)} task(s) reaped: {reaped}")
        except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001
            log(f"S3.4 reaper pass error (cycle {cycle}): {exc!r}")
        if now - last_drift_at >= drift_interval_s:
            last_drift_at = now
            check_drift(
                runtime,
                now,
                cap_bytes,
                base=base,
                log=log,
            )
        write_health(
            health_dir, build_runtime_health(cfg, runtime, main_ok)
        )
        if settle_s > 0 and (time.monotonic() - t0) >= settle_s:
            log(f"probe loop: settled {settle_s:.0f}s (cycle {cycle}), exit 0")
            return EXIT_OK
        # S2.2: health_poll_s cadence (the steady-loop constant from
        # serving.yaml, or the BONSAI2_HEALTH_POLL_S test hook).
        time.sleep(health_poll_s)
        cycle += 1


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# entry point
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="bonsai2 instance manager (MAP 2.1–2.11, S2.1–S2.8)."
    )
    ap.add_argument(
        "--settle",
        type=float,
        default=0.0,
        help="after boot + notify, run the probe loop this many "
        "seconds then exit 0 (0 = run forever under systemd)",
    )
    ap.add_argument(
        "--config-dir",
        default=None,
        help=f"dir holding subagent-engine.yaml + serving.yaml "
        f"(default {DEFAULT_CONFIG_DIR}; "
        f"or set BONSAI2_ENGINE_YAML / BONSAI2_SERVING_YAML)",
    )
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)

    cfg_dir = args.config_dir or DEFAULT_CONFIG_DIR
    engine_yaml = Path(
        os.environ.get(
            "BONSAI2_ENGINE_YAML", str(Path(cfg_dir) / "subagent-engine.yaml")
        )
    )
    serving_yaml = Path(
        os.environ.get("BONSAI2_SERVING_YAML", str(Path(cfg_dir) / "serving.yaml"))
    )
    health_dir = Path(os.environ.get("BONSAI2_HEALTH_DIR", DEFAULT_HEALTH_DIR))

    notify_sd("STATUS=bonsai2-manager: loading config")
    try:
        cfg = load_config(Path(engine_yaml), Path(serving_yaml))
    except ValueError as exc:
        _log(str(exc))
        notify_sd(f"STATUS=bonsai2-manager: {exc}")
        notify_sd("STOPPING=1")  # pre-READY exit (G3 PR #10 r10 P2)
        return EXIT_CONFIG

    n_yaml = int(cfg["count"])
    budget_gb = float(cfg["budget_gb"])

    # --- S2.8 #1 shared whole-boot deadline (STARTS NOW) ----------------
    # ONE shared budget for the whole boot: every step from here — the
    # S2.3 reads (MemTotal, the main cgroup read incl. its docker
    # inspect), the S1.4 footprint, the S2.1 main probe, and the S2.5
    # spawn sequence — counts against boot_target_s. G3 PR #10 r14 P1
    # moved the start before the main probe; r15 P1 moves it before the
    # S2.3 reads too, so an un-bounded docker inspect or a wedged cgroup
    # read lives INSIDE the budget, not on top of it. (S2.8 #1: 'Cold
    # boot → main + N healthy ≤ 180 s'.)
    boot_deadline_s = time.monotonic() + cfg["boot_target_s"]
    boot_t0 = time.monotonic()  # MAP 2.9: the MEASURED boot's T

    # --- S2.3 boot-time budget check -----------------------------------
    try:
        total_gb = read_total_gb()
    except OSError as exc:
        # A boot-tier /proc/meminfo read failure is a config/boot-environment
        # problem, not a transient one: map it to ERR_CONFIG (exit 2) instead
        # of an uncaught traceback (exit 1), matching the load_config contract.
        _log(f"ERR_CONFIG: MemTotal read failed: {exc}")
        notify_sd(f"STATUS=bonsai2-manager: ERR_CONFIG: {exc}")
        notify_sd("STOPPING=1")  # pre-READY exit (G3 PR #10 r10 P2)
        return EXIT_CONFIG
    main_used, source = main_used_gb(cfg["main_container"], cfg["main_footprint_gb"])
    _log(
        f"S2.3: total {total_gb:.2f} GB, main {main_used:.2f} GB ({source}), "
        f"os-reserved {cfg['os_reserved_gb']:.1f} GB, N={n_yaml}, budget {budget_gb:.2f} GB"
    )
    n_prime, free_gb, warns = planning_tier(
        total_gb, main_used, cfg["os_reserved_gb"], n_yaml, budget_gb
    )
    for w in warns:
        _log(w)
    _log(f"S2.3: free {free_gb:.2f} GB -> N'={n_prime}")

    # --- S1.4 allocation-tier need (max(footprint + 1.0, cap)) --------
    # cfg["per_slot_per_token_gb"] is the S1.4 KV constant for the
    # configured kv_cache dtype (r13 P1: the S1.6 key feeds the
    # footprint; load_config ERR_CONFIGs a dtype without a locked value).
    footprint = bonsai2_vram.instance_footprint_gb(
        cfg["weights_gb"],
        int(cfg["slots"]),
        int(cfg["ctx_size"]),
        cfg["per_slot_per_token_gb"],
    )
    # Pinned single source of truth (G3 PR #10 r14 P2): the tier need is
    # derived from the SAME `footprint` value the log prints — no second
    # independent recomputation that could silently diverge (the
    # allocation_need_gb module function stays for the pure-formula
    # tests; main() is the live caller).
    need_gb = max(footprint + TIER_MARGIN_GB, budget_gb)
    _log(
        f"S1.4 allocation tier need: {need_gb:.2f} GB "
        f"(max(footprint {footprint:.2f} + {TIER_MARGIN_GB:.1f} GB margin, "
        f"cap {budget_gb:.2f} GB); kv_cache={cfg['kv_cache']})"
    )

    # --- S2.1 boot order: main stack first -----------------------------
    main_ok = main_healthy(cfg["main_endpoint"])
    _log(
        f"S2.1: main endpoint {cfg['main_endpoint']} healthy={main_ok} "
        f"(recorded in health.json; the pool is an independent engine, "
        f"Wants= not Requires=)"
    )

    # --- S2.4 N=0 PTQ1_0 fallback (MAP 2.7) / S2.5 spawn path (ordered) --
    sysctl_base = systemctl_base()
    ctx_in_use = int(cfg["ctx_size"])
    if n_prime == 0 and fallback_quant_available(cfg):
        # S2.4 (S2.3 entry, verbatim): 'install retries once with the
        # PTQ1_0 fallback (S1.1, ~18% smaller)'. The retry re-plans at
        # the fallback budget, rewrites instance 0's unit (model +
        # memory flags) in place, and runs the S1.4 allocation tier +
        # the S2.5 spawn for that one instance — with the S2.4 ctx-halve
        # retry inside (a tier fail at the default ctx; S1.4's exit 4
        # applies only if THAT also fails).
        exit_code, spawned, detail, start_rc, ctx_in_use = fallback_retry(
            cfg,
            engine_yaml,
            sysctl_base,
            boot_deadline_s,
            log=_log,
        )
        if exit_code == EXIT_OK and spawned:
            n_prime = 1
    else:
        exit_code, spawned, detail, start_rc = spawn_sequence(
            cfg,
            n_prime,
            need_gb,
            log=_log,
            sysctl_base=sysctl_base,
            boot_deadline_s=boot_deadline_s,
        )
    _log(detail)

    # --- i = 0 allocation OOM (exit 4): a BOOT-TIME failure -------------
    # spawn_sequence returns (4, [], detail, {}) — nothing started, the
    # manager exits BEFORE any READY=1 (the S1.4 "exits 4 before the
    # instance's KV allocation" contract). Handle it here, explicitly, so
    # the exit-4 path is not "falls through with an empty state":
    #   * health.json is written with 0 instances. stack_summary returns
    #     "main-only" for 0 instances (there really are no subagents
    #     running), and main_only_reason names the CAUSE — here
    #     "ERR_VRAM_OOM" so a reader of bonsai2-health can distinguish
    #     "planning tier found nothing to run" (budget_zero / user_unloaded)
    #     from "planning tier granted, allocation tier refused" (this path).
    #     The S3.1 schema keeps main_only_reason free-form for 0-instance
    #     documents (S2.4 amendment 2026-09-23 already opened it to new
    #     reason values like the MAP 2.7 fallback_zero candidate).
    #   * STATUS= is emitted so systemd's journal shows WHY the start
    #     failed (without it, Type=notify + exit 4 = a bare "status=failed
    #     reason=exit-code" with no context)
    #   * then exit 4, which systemd under Type=notify treats as a FAILED
    #     START -> Restart=on-failure + RestartSec=5 (the unit's policy).
    if exit_code == EXIT_VRAM_OOM:
        _log("S1.4: i=0 allocation OOM -> exit 4 (ERR_VRAM_OOM), no READY sent")
        # G3 PR #10 r15 P2: the S2.1 main_ok was probed BEFORE the spawn
        # sequence — re-probe now, so the OOM health document reports the
        # main stack's CURRENT state (a main that died during the tier
        # rejection is not silently recorded as healthy).
        main_ok_now = main_healthy(cfg["main_endpoint"])
        write_health(health_dir, build_health(cfg, [], main_ok_now, "ERR_VRAM_OOM"))
        notify_sd(f"STATUS=bonsai2-manager: {detail}")
        # Pre-READY exit: emit STOPPING= so a `systemctl stop` issued during
        # this still-"starting" window has Type=notify bookkeeping to
        # complete against instead of falling through to the TimeoutStopSec
        # main-PID path (G3 PR #10 r10 P2). The normal case (boot exits 4
        # in ~seconds) is a FAILED START -> Restart=on-failure, not a stop.
        notify_sd("STOPPING=1")
        return EXIT_VRAM_OOM

    # --- S2.4 N=0 / main-only mode (MAP 2.7) ----------------------------
    # The PTQ1_0 fallback already ran above (it is the FIRST move when
    # N'=0 at the PQ2_0 budget + a fallback size is configured); a
    # fallback OOM was handled by the exit-4 path above. What remains:
    #   * no fallback size in the config -> plain main-only (P1,
    #     G3 PR #10 r4);
    #   * a fallback that could not run -> exit 4 (S2.4 live-margin
    #     OOM), already returned above.
    main_only_reason: str | None = None
    if n_prime == 0:
        # S2.4: the shared reason is `budget_zero` (the spec shares it
        # with the post-PTQ1_0-retry zero — S2.4 amendment 2026-09-23,
        # G3 PR #10 round 2; a distinct `fallback_zero` distinguishing
        # 'PTQ1_0 was tried and still 0' is a MAP 2.7 candidate the
        # S3.1 schema is open to). Distinct from S3.2's `user_unloaded`
        # (explicit unload).
        main_only_reason = "budget_zero"
        _log(
            "S2.4: N'=0 -> main-only mode (reason=budget_zero)."
            + (
                ""
                if fallback_quant_available(cfg)
                else " No model.fallback_size_bytes in the YAML — the "
                "PTQ1_0 retry had no size to plan against (P1, G3 PR #10 r4)."
            )
        )
        boot_states: list[str] = []
        write_health(
            health_dir,
            build_health(cfg, boot_states, main_ok, main_only_reason=main_only_reason),
        )
        notify_sd("STATUS=bonsai2-manager: main-only (budget_zero)")
        runtime = Runtime(cfg, [], boot_states)
        runtime.main_only_reason = main_only_reason
        if args.settle > 0:
            # The settle path exits 0 after settle_s, but still marks
            # readiness FIRST: under a user-scope Type=notify start (the
            # @gpu harness shape) an exit-0 with no prior READY=1 is a
            # FAILED START in systemd notify semantics — consistent with
            # the non-settle path below and the instances settle path
            # (G3 PR #10 round 5 P1).
            notify_sd("READY=1")
            return run_probe_loop(cfg, runtime, health_dir, args.settle, log=_log)
        notify_sd("READY=1")
        return EXIT_OK

    # --- first health.json (states from the spawn outcome) -------------
    # A unit whose `systemctl start` returned non-zero reflects its S1.5
    # bfailed path DIRECTLY in health.json (the start_rc the spawn
    # sequence surfaces) instead of re-deriving by probing a possibly-dead
    # unit. A clean start that has not gone healthy yet is `degraded`
    # (alive-but-unproven), not bfailed.
    boot_states = initial_states(spawned, start_rc, cfg)
    write_health(
        health_dir, build_health(cfg, boot_states, main_ok, main_only_reason)
    )
    notify_sd(f"STATUS=bonsai2-manager: booted {len(spawned)} instance(s)")

    # The steady loop owns per-instance state from here (MAP 2.6/2.8/
    # 2.10): one entry per configured index, the boot-time states seeded
    # onto the spawned prefix, the S2.4 main-only reason only when the
    # running set is empty (the main-only boot path set it; a partial
    # load still has >=1 instance so the S3.1 reason stays absent).
    runtime = Runtime(cfg, spawned, boot_states)
    if not spawned:
        runtime.main_only_reason = main_only_reason
    # S2.4 (MAP 2.7): a fallback boot ran instance 0 at ctx_in_use
    # (the default, or the halved ctx after the ctx-halve retry) with
    # the PTQ1_0 quant — seed the LIVE value + ctx_reduced on the
    # steady-loop entry so health.json reports the ctx the unit ACTUALLY
    # runs (S3.1 `ctx` = the live value, `ctx_reduced` marks the halve).
    if ctx_in_use != int(cfg["ctx_size"]):
        for i in spawned:
            e = runtime.entry(i)
            if e is not None:
                e.ctx = ctx_in_use
                e.ctx_reduced = True

    # S2.6 (MAP 2.9): `boot_target_s` is measured, not guessed — write
    # the MEASURED boot back to serving.yaml (the S2.7 reboot test's T,
    # recorded by the manager at every boot that spawned >=1 instance:
    # a main-only boot measured nothing to target). Best-effort: a
    # missing/unwritable file is logged, never fatal (the S2.6 file is
    # install.sh's; the manager's job is to keep its measurement fresh).
    if spawned:
        measured_boot_s = time.monotonic() - boot_t0
        write_boot_target_s(serving_yaml, measured_boot_s, log=_log)

    if args.settle > 0:
        # Readiness before the settle window: same user-scope Type=notify
        # FAILED-START rationale as the main-only settle branch above
        # (G3 PR #10 round 5 P1) — a settle run is a normal, ready boot.
        notify_sd("READY=1")
        return run_probe_loop(cfg, runtime, health_dir, args.settle, log=_log)

    # systemd Type=notify: signal readiness, then run the probe loop forever.
    notify_sd("READY=1")
    run_probe_loop(cfg, runtime, health_dir, 0.0, log=_log)
    # Unreachable in production: run_probe_loop(settle_s=0.0) is an infinite
    # loop — the process ends by systemd's SIGTERM (a normal stop, not a
    # failure; READY=1 was already sent). EXIT_OK (not a boot-path variable):
    # the exit-4 boot path already returned, so by the time we reach the
    # probe loop the spawn outcome is always the normal 0 (G3 PR #10 r6 P2;
    # variable renamed r11 P2 for reader clarity).
    return EXIT_OK


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
